ForgeData.register("media",{"MEDIA-1":{"label":"Theoretical Framework","color":"#7c2d12","questions":[{"id":"MED-01","stem":"Stuart Hall's encoding/decoding model argues that audiences:","options":{"A":"Actively decode media texts, and the meaning created depends on the viewer's cultural background — producers encode a preferred reading, but audiences may adopt a negotiated or oppositional reading.","B":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","C":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","D":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Hall (1980) encoding/decoding: producers encode a preferred reading using dominant ideology. Audiences decode from their own cultural position: (1) Dominant/hegemonic — accepts preferred meaning fully; (2) Negotiated — partially accepts, modifies from own position; (3) Oppositional — rejects preferred reading, substitutes their own. Key point: meaning is not fixed in the text but created in the interaction between text and reader. Exam: apply this when analysing audience response, not just representation.","reforge":{"stem":"A news broadcast frames immigration negatively. A viewer who is themselves an immigrant rejects this framing and sees the report as biased. Hall would describe this as:","options":{"A":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","B":"Production budget","C":"An oppositional reading — the viewer's social position (as an immigrant) leads them to reject the preferred meaning and substitute an alternative interpretation based on their own experience.","D":"Reception theory"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"MEDIA-1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-02","stem":"Curran and Seaton's power without responsibility model argues that:","options":{"A":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","B":"The concentration of media ownership in the hands of a few large conglomerates limits the range of ideas and viewpoints in circulation, serving the interests of powerful owners rather than the public.","C":"Genre convention","D":"Narrative order"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Curran & Seaton: media ownership concentrated in a few powerful companies (News Corp, Disney, Comcast). This concentration: reduces diversity of voices; creates commercial pressure prioritising profit over quality/public interest; enables owners to influence editorial line (Murdoch and The Sun's political positions). BUT: Curran also notes digital media has created some diversity (YouTube, podcasting). Hesmondhalgh critique: market logic dominates cultural industries regardless of ownership structure. Exam: evaluate — does concentration always limit diversity?","reforge":{"stem":"A media conglomerate that owns a news channel, a film studio and several newspapers is an example of:","options":{"A":"Lighting codes","B":"a small independent production","C":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","D":"Conglomeration — ownership across multiple different media sectors, creating cross-promotional synergy and market dominance. Curran & Seaton argue this limits diversity by concentrating power in fewer hands."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"MEDIA-1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-03","stem":"Gerbner's cultivation theory argues that:","options":{"A":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"Long-term, heavy exposure to television cultivates a shared worldview — heavy viewers develop a distorted perception of social reality that more closely matches the TV world than actual social statistics, particularly regarding crime and danger (the 'mean world syndrome').","D":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Gerbner (1970s+) cultivation theory: TV is the dominant storytelling medium. Heavy viewers (4+ hours/day) develop TV-cultivated perceptions: overestimate crime rates, police violence prevalence, and physical danger. 'Mean world syndrome' — heavy viewers more fearful, more conservative about crime. First-order effects (facts) and second-order effects (values/attitudes). Mainstreaming — heavy viewing homogenises diverse groups toward TV's dominant values. Criticisms: correlation not causation; people choose programmes (reinforcement not cultivation); ignores active audience; pre-digital TV environment.","reforge":{"stem":"Research finds heavy TV viewers consistently overestimate the percentage of the population who are criminals. Gerbner would explain this as:","options":{"A":"Cultivation — heavy TV exposure leads viewers to perceive social reality as matching TV's overrepresentation of crime, producing first-order cultivation effects on factual beliefs about the world.","B":"The hypodermic model","C":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","D":"Production budget"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"MEDIA-1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-04","stem":"In media studies, 'representation' means:","options":{"A":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","B":"a completely unique character","C":"Distribution channel","D":"The process by which media construct versions of reality — selecting, combining and presenting elements to produce meanings about social groups, events and ideas. Representations are constructions, not reflections, of reality."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Representation: media don't reflect reality — they RE-present it through selection and construction. Key ideas: (1) Re-presentation — same reality can be constructed differently (tabloid vs broadsheet coverage of same event). (2) Stereotyping — shortcut representations that can reinforce or challenge ideological assumptions. (3) Power — who controls representation? Whose perspective is centred? (4) Stuart Hall's 'the spectacle of the other' — difference is marked, exoticised, othered. Exam always asks for application to specific texts — don't just define, analyse HOW the text constructs representation.","reforge":{"stem":"Two newspapers cover the same protest: one uses the headline 'Passionate campaigners demand action' and another uses 'Troublemakers disrupt city'. This illustrates which concept?","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"Re-presentation — the same event is constructed differently through language choices, encoding different preferred readings about protesters and their legitimacy. Neither is a neutral 'reflection' of the event.","C":"Genre convention","D":"Cultivation only"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"MEDIA-1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-05","stem":"Livingstone and Lunt's critique of regulation argues that:","options":{"A":"Traditional media regulation models — designed for broadcast media with clear national gatekeepers — are inadequate for converged, digital, global media environments where audiences are also producers and national boundaries are meaningless.","B":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","C":"a restriction on digital distribution","D":"Vertical editing"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Livingstone & Lunt (2011): traditional regulation framework built on broadcast model (few producers, many passive consumers, national transmission). Digital convergence undermines this: (1) Audiences become producers (UGC — YouTube, TikTok); (2) Global platforms (Netflix, Google) operate across jurisdictions; (3) Algorithmic curation not 'editorial' in traditional sense; (4) Speed of content creation outpaces regulatory response. Regulatory bodies: Ofcom (UK broadcasting + telecoms), IPSO (press), BBFC (film classification), ASA (advertising). OSA 2023 — new attempt to regulate online harms. Exam: evaluate whether any regulatory model can address these challenges.","reforge":{"stem":"A teenager is exposed to harmful content via a recommendation algorithm on a social media platform. This situation most clearly illustrates:","options":{"A":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","B":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","C":"Livingstone and Lunt's argument that traditional national regulation cannot govern global digital platforms operating across jurisdictions.","D":"Cultivation theory — long-term algorithmic exposure shapes users' worldview."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"MEDIA-1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-06","stem":"Van Zoonen's feminist media theory argues that:","options":{"A":"Distribution channel","B":"Gender is a cultural construction performed and perpetuated through representation — media texts produce and reproduce gendered identities by consistently presenting femininity and masculinity in particular ways, often through the 'male gaze'.","C":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Van Zoonen (1994) feminist media theory: gender is not natural but constructed through cultural practices including media representation. The 'male gaze' (Mulvey — applied to film originally): camera positions women as spectacle for male viewer's pleasure. Women coded as objects rather than subjects. BUT: Van Zoonen acknowledges female audiences actively resist and renegotiate representations. Contemporary relevance: online content creation, OnlyFans debate — exploitation or empowerment? Intersectionality (Crenshaw) — gender intersects with race, class in representations. Exam: avoid stating all representation is negative — evaluate complexity.","reforge":{"stem":"A music video features a female artist singing from a stationary position while the camera repeatedly focuses on her body in fragmented close-ups. Van Zoonen and Mulvey would analyse this as:","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"Genre convention","C":"Cultivation only","D":"An example of the male gaze — the camera's fragmentation of the female body positions her as visual spectacle for a presumed male viewer's pleasure rather than as a subject with agency, reproducing a patriarchal representation of femininity."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"MEDIA-1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-07","stem":"The 'hypodermic needle' model of media effects assumes:","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","C":"Media messages are injected directly into passive audiences who absorb them uncritically, producing immediate, uniform behavioural effects — a simple, direct cause-and-effect relationship.","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Hypodermic needle (magic bullet) model: early effects theory (1920s-40s). Assumes: audiences are passive, homogeneous, isolated from social ties; media has direct, powerful, immediate effects. Associated with: Bandura's Bobo doll experiments (children imitated modelled aggression); moral panics about film violence. Criticisms: (1) audiences are NOT passive — two-step flow (Katz & Lazarsfeld), active audience theory; (2) social context mediates effects; (3) laboratory conditions are artificial; (4) correlation ≠ causation. Modern relevance: still cited in political debates (video games/violence) despite weak evidence. Exam: most useful as a model to critique rather than apply.","reforge":{"stem":"Research attempting to prove that violent video games cause violent behaviour repeatedly fails to establish a causal link. This most directly undermines:","options":{"A":"The hypodermic needle model — which predicts direct, measurable behavioural effects from media exposure. The absence of clear causal evidence suggests audiences are not the passive receptacles this model assumes.","B":"Production budget","C":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","D":"Reception theory"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"MEDIA-1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-08","stem":"Blumler and Katz's uses and gratifications theory argues that:","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"The hypodermic model","C":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","D":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Blumler & Katz (1974) uses and gratifications: shifts focus from 'what does media DO to audiences?' to 'what do audiences DO with media?' Four needs: (1) Diversion/escape — entertainment, relaxation; (2) Personal relationships — companionship, social interaction via media (parasocial relationships); (3) Personal identity — finding values and role models in texts; (4) Surveillance/information — understanding the world. Modern examples: binge-watching (escape), following influencers (parasocial), using TikTok for news. Criticism: assumes audiences are always rational and purposeful; ignores ideological effects; ignores how industry shapes 'choice'.","reforge":{"stem":"A student watches a reality TV show featuring young people from a similar background and identifies with a contestant who overcomes adversity. Uses and gratifications would explain this as:","options":{"A":"Production budget","B":"The personal identity function — the student is actively using the text to explore and reinforce their own values and sense of self by identifying with a character whose experiences resonate with their own.","C":"Reception theory","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"MEDIA-1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-01","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"In semiotic analysis, a sign is:","options":{"A":"something that communicates a meaning","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"Institutional ownership","D":"A mode of address"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Signs include words, images, sounds and conventions that audiences interpret.","reforge":{"stem":"A red heart icon is used to represent love in an advert. In semiotics, the icon is a:","options":{"A":"Sign","B":"Ownership pattern","C":"A rating","D":"Demographic"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-02","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Anchorage is used to:","options":{"A":"Institutional ownership","B":"limit or direct the meaning of an image","C":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","D":"Ownership pattern"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Captions and headlines can guide how an image is read.","reforge":{"stem":"A photograph of a protest is printed beside the headline 'Peaceful citizens demand change'. What does the headline provide?","options":{"A":"A rating","B":"Anchorage","C":"Convergence","D":"A mode of address"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-03","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Denotation refers to:","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"Institutional ownership","C":"the literal or descriptive meaning of a media sign","D":"A mode of address"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Denotation is distinguished from connotation, which concerns associated meanings.","reforge":{"stem":"When analysing an image, a student lists the objects and colours that are literally visible before discussing meanings. This is describing:","options":{"A":"A rating","B":"Ownership pattern","C":"Denotation","D":"Vertical integration"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-04","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media stereotype is:","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"a completely unique character","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"a simplified and repeated representation of a group"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Stereotypes can make groups seem fixed, natural or easily recognisable.","reforge":{"stem":"An advert repeatedly presents scientists as older men in white coats. This is an example of a media:","options":{"A":"Cultivation only","B":"Genre convention","C":"Distribution channel","D":"Stereotype"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-05","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Hall's representation theory argues that media representations:","options":{"A":"construct meaning rather than simply mirror reality","B":"Distribution channel","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Representation selects, frames and organises meanings through codes and conventions.","reforge":{"stem":"A news drama selects some groups as threatening and others as respectable. Hall's approach would say the representation is:","options":{"A":"Constructed through selection and ideology","B":"Genre convention","C":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","D":"Cultivation only"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-06","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The preferred reading of a media text is:","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"the interpretation most encouraged by its creators","C":"the geography of a whole setting","D":"removes the need for marketing"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Audiences may accept, negotiate or oppose a preferred reading.","reforge":{"stem":"A documentary is encoded to encourage viewers to accept its environmental argument. The reading most encouraged by its producers is the:","options":{"A":"Oppositional reading","B":"Preferred reading","C":"Negotiated rejection","D":"Denotative code"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-07","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media genre is useful because it:","options":{"A":"the geography of a whole setting","B":"removes the need for marketing","C":"sets recognisable conventions and expectations","D":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Genres help producers and audiences organise expectations, while texts can subvert them.","reforge":{"stem":"A streaming platform groups crime dramas together so audiences know what style and conventions to expect. This illustrates the usefulness of:","options":{"A":"A public service announcement","B":"Naturalistic underexposure","C":"Genre","D":"Oppositional reading"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-08","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A high-key lighting style commonly suggests:","options":{"A":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","B":"the geography of a whole setting","C":"removes the need for marketing","D":"brightness, openness or a positive mood"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Lighting meaning depends on context but high-key images often appear bright and accessible.","reforge":{"stem":"A cheerful advert uses bright, even lighting with very few shadows. Which lighting style is being used?","options":{"A":"Low-key chiaroscuro","B":"Naturalistic underexposure","C":"Low-key lighting","D":"High-key lighting"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-09","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A close-up is often used to emphasise:","options":{"A":"facial expression or emotional detail","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"the geography of a whole setting","D":"removes the need for marketing"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Shot size directs attention and shapes audience involvement.","reforge":{"stem":"A film cuts tightly to a character's eyes as they hear unexpected news. What is the main effect of this shot?","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"It emphasises emotion and reaction","C":"Naturalistic underexposure","D":"Oppositional reading"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-10","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A non-linear narrative presents events:","options":{"A":"the geography of a whole setting","B":"out of chronological order","C":"Negotiated rejection","D":"Low-key chiaroscuro"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Flashbacks and parallel timelines can make audiences reconstruct narrative order.","reforge":{"stem":"A thriller begins with the final confrontation, then returns to earlier events to explain it. Its narrative is:","options":{"A":"secrecy and menace in every context","B":"A public service announcement","C":"Non-linear","D":"A genre institution"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-11","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media institution is:","options":{"A":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","B":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","C":"an organisation involved in producing, distributing or regulating media","D":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Institutions operate within economic, technological and regulatory contexts.","reforge":{"stem":"A company that funds, produces and distributes television programmes is a media:","options":{"A":"The process by which audiences become more homogeneous in their media consumption habits.","B":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","C":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","D":"Institution"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-12","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Vertical integration occurs when a company controls:","options":{"A":"Self-regulation by media companies always protects the public interest.","B":"only two unrelated products","C":"User-generated content","D":"different stages of production and distribution"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Control across stages can reduce costs and dependence on other firms.","reforge":{"stem":"A media corporation buys a production studio and a streaming platform in its supply chain. This is:","options":{"A":"Vertical integration","B":"Horizontal audience fragmentation","C":"Public service broadcasting","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-13","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A public service broadcaster is expected to:","options":{"A":"serve wider public purposes rather than only maximise profit","B":"Public service broadcasting","C":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","D":"Horizontal audience fragmentation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"PSB obligations can include range, quality, diversity and access.","reforge":{"stem":"A broadcaster is required to provide news, education and content for minority audiences rather than only chase profit. This reflects a:","options":{"A":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","B":"Public service remit","C":"The process by which audiences become more homogeneous in their media consumption habits.","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-14","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media conglomerate is:","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"a large company with interests across media markets","C":"Lighting codes","D":"a genre convention"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Conglomerates may own multiple brands and platforms.","reforge":{"stem":"A corporation owns newspapers, film studios, radio stations and online platforms. It is a media:","options":{"A":"Genre convention","B":"Narrative order","C":"Conglomerate","D":"Single-text audience"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-15","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Media convergence describes:","options":{"A":"a restriction on digital distribution","B":"Vertical editing","C":"the coming together of technologies, industries or media forms","D":"Self-regulation by media companies always protects the public interest."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Convergence can change production, circulation and consumption.","reforge":{"stem":"A smartphone combines filming, editing, publishing and social interaction in one device. This is media:","options":{"A":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","B":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","C":"Linear narration","D":"Convergence"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-16","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media text's mode of address refers to:","options":{"A":"Cultivation theory — long-term algorithmic exposure shapes users' worldview.","B":"Ownership pattern","C":"the ownership of a camera","D":"how it positions and speaks to its audience"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Language, tone and design create a relationship with an implied audience.","reforge":{"stem":"A presenter uses informal language and direct address to make viewers feel like friends. This describes the text's:","options":{"A":"Institutional ownership","B":"Mode of address","C":"A mode of address","D":"A rating"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E1-17","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"An audience demographic is based on characteristics such as:","options":{"A":"age, gender, income or education","B":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","C":"Primary institution","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Demographics help institutions identify and sell to audience groups.","reforge":{"stem":"An audience profile records viewers' age, gender, income and location. These are examples of:","options":{"A":"media products cannot be targeted","B":"A production code","C":"Demographic characteristics","D":"A stereotype"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-001","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Stuart Hall's encoding/decoding model argues that audiences: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"Actively decode media texts, and the meaning created depends on the viewer's cultural background — producers encode a preferred reading, but audiences may adopt a negotiated or oppositional reading.","C":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","D":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Hall (1980) encoding/decoding: producers encode a preferred reading using dominant ideology. Audiences decode from their own cultural position: (1) Dominant/hegemonic — accepts preferred meaning fully; (2) Negotiated — partially accepts, modifies from own position; (3) Oppositional — rejects preferred reading, substitutes their own. Key point: meaning is not fixed in the text but created in the interaction between text and reader. Exam: apply this when analysing audience response, not just representation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A news broadcast frames immigration negatively. A viewer who is themselves an immigrant rejects this framing and sees the report as biased. Hall would describe this as:","options":{"A":"Production budget","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"Reception theory","D":"An oppositional reading — the viewer's social position (as an immigrant) leads them to reject the preferred meaning and substitute an alternative interpretation based on their own experience."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-003","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Gerbner's cultivation theory argues that: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"Long-term, heavy exposure to television cultivates a shared worldview — heavy viewers develop a distorted perception of social reality that more closely matches the TV world than actual social statistics, particularly regarding crime and danger (the 'mean world syndrome').","D":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Gerbner (1970s+) cultivation theory: TV is the dominant storytelling medium. Heavy viewers (4+ hours/day) develop TV-cultivated perceptions: overestimate crime rates, police violence prevalence, and physical danger. 'Mean world syndrome' — heavy viewers more fearful, more conservative about crime. First-order effects (facts) and second-order effects (values/attitudes). Mainstreaming — heavy viewing homogenises diverse groups toward TV's dominant values. Criticisms: correlation not causation; people choose programmes (reinforcement not cultivation); ignores active audience; pre-digital TV environment.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Research finds heavy TV viewers consistently overestimate the percentage of the population who are criminals. Gerbner would explain this as:","options":{"A":"Cultivation — heavy TV exposure leads viewers to perceive social reality as matching TV's overrepresentation of crime, producing first-order cultivation effects on factual beliefs about the world.","B":"The hypodermic model","C":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","D":"Production budget"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-005","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Livingstone and Lunt's critique of regulation argues that: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"a restriction on digital distribution","B":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","C":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","D":"Traditional media regulation models — designed for broadcast media with clear national gatekeepers — are inadequate for converged, digital, global media environments where audiences are also producers and national boundaries are meaningless."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Livingstone & Lunt (2011): traditional regulation framework built on broadcast model (few producers, many passive consumers, national transmission). Digital convergence undermines this: (1) Audiences become producers (UGC — YouTube, TikTok); (2) Global platforms (Netflix, Google) operate across jurisdictions; (3) Algorithmic curation not 'editorial' in traditional sense; (4) Speed of content creation outpaces regulatory response. Regulatory bodies: Ofcom (UK broadcasting + telecoms), IPSO (press), BBFC (film classification), ASA (advertising). OSA 2023 — new attempt to regulate online harms. Exam: evaluate whether any regulatory model can address these challenges.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A teenager is exposed to harmful content via a recommendation algorithm on a social media platform. This situation most clearly illustrates:","options":{"A":"Vertical editing","B":"Livingstone and Lunt's argument that traditional national regulation cannot govern global digital platforms operating across jurisdictions.","C":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","D":"Cultivation theory — long-term algorithmic exposure shapes users' worldview."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-007","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The 'hypodermic needle' model of media effects assumes: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"Media messages are injected directly into passive audiences who absorb them uncritically, producing immediate, uniform behavioural effects — a simple, direct cause-and-effect relationship.","B":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","C":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Hypodermic needle (magic bullet) model: early effects theory (1920s-40s). Assumes: audiences are passive, homogeneous, isolated from social ties; media has direct, powerful, immediate effects. Associated with: Bandura's Bobo doll experiments (children imitated modelled aggression); moral panics about film violence. Criticisms: (1) audiences are NOT passive — two-step flow (Katz & Lazarsfeld), active audience theory; (2) social context mediates effects; (3) laboratory conditions are artificial; (4) correlation ≠ causation. Modern relevance: still cited in political debates (video games/violence) despite weak evidence. Exam: most useful as a model to critique rather than apply.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Research attempting to prove that violent video games cause violent behaviour repeatedly fails to establish a causal link. This most directly undermines:","options":{"A":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","B":"Production budget","C":"The hypodermic needle model — which predicts direct, measurable behavioural effects from media exposure. The absence of clear causal evidence suggests audiences are not the passive receptacles this model assumes.","D":"Reception theory"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-009","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"In semiotic analysis, a sign is: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","B":"something that communicates a meaning","C":"Institutional ownership","D":"A mode of address"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Signs include words, images, sounds and conventions that audiences interpret.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A red heart icon is used to represent love in an advert. In semiotics, the icon is a:","options":{"A":"Sign","B":"Ownership pattern","C":"A rating","D":"Demographic"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-011","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"denotation refers to: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"Institutional ownership","C":"the literal or descriptive meaning of a media sign","D":"A mode of address"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Denotation is distinguished from connotation, which concerns associated meanings.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"When analysing an image, a student lists the objects and colours that are literally visible before discussing meanings. This is describing:","options":{"A":"A rating","B":"Ownership pattern","C":"Denotation","D":"Vertical integration"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-013","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Hall's representation theory argues that media representations: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"Distribution channel","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"construct meaning rather than simply mirror reality"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Representation selects, frames and organises meanings through codes and conventions.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A news drama selects some groups as threatening and others as respectable. Hall's approach would say the representation is:","options":{"A":"Constructed through selection and ideology","B":"Genre convention","C":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","D":"Cultivation only"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-015","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media genre is useful because it: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"sets recognisable conventions and expectations","B":"removes the need for marketing","C":"the geography of a whole setting","D":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Genres help producers and audiences organise expectations, while texts can subvert them.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A streaming platform groups crime dramas together so audiences know what style and conventions to expect. This illustrates the usefulness of:","options":{"A":"A public service announcement","B":"Naturalistic underexposure","C":"Genre","D":"Oppositional reading"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-017","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A close-up is often used to emphasise: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"A public service announcement","B":"facial expression or emotional detail","C":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","D":"the geography of a whole setting"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Shot size directs attention and shapes audience involvement.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A film cuts tightly to a character's eyes as they hear unexpected news. What is the main effect of this shot?","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"It emphasises emotion and reaction","C":"Naturalistic underexposure","D":"Oppositional reading"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-019","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media institution is: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","B":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","C":"an organisation involved in producing, distributing or regulating media","D":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Institutions operate within economic, technological and regulatory contexts.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A company that funds, produces and distributes television programmes is a media:","options":{"A":"The process by which audiences become more homogeneous in their media consumption habits.","B":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","C":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","D":"Institution"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-021","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A public service broadcaster is expected to: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","B":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","C":"Horizontal audience fragmentation","D":"serve wider public purposes rather than only maximise profit"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"PSB obligations can include range, quality, diversity and access.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A broadcaster is required to provide news, education and content for minority audiences rather than only chase profit. This reflects a:","options":{"A":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","B":"Public service remit","C":"The process by which audiences become more homogeneous in their media consumption habits.","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-023","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Media convergence describes: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"the coming together of technologies, industries or media forms","B":"Vertical editing","C":"a restriction on digital distribution","D":"Self-regulation by media companies always protects the public interest."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Convergence can change production, circulation and consumption.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A smartphone combines filming, editing, publishing and social interaction in one device. This is media:","options":{"A":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","B":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","C":"Linear narration","D":"Convergence"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-025","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"An audience demographic is based on characteristics such as: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"age, gender, income or education","C":"Primary institution","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Demographics help institutions identify and sell to audience groups.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"An audience profile records viewers' age, gender, income and location. These are examples of:","options":{"A":"media products cannot be targeted","B":"A production code","C":"Demographic characteristics","D":"A stereotype"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-027","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The concept of 'synergy' in the media industry refers to:","options":{"A":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","B":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","C":"The mutual promotion of products across a conglomerate's holdings — where one product markets another across different media platforms, multiplying revenue from a single IP.","D":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Synergy: when a conglomerate's various arms reinforce each other commercially. Example: Marvel Cinematic Universe — Disney produces the film (studio), releases on Disney+ (streaming), sells merchandise through Disney stores, promotes via ABC News (TV), covers in Marvel comics (publishing). Each outlet promotes the others — the whole is worth more than the sum of its parts. This is why conglomerates acquire across media types, not just depth. Exam link: synergy benefits large conglomerates, making it harder for independent producers to compete — connects to Curran & Seaton's diversity concerns.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Disney releases a new superhero film, simultaneously launching a Disney+ tie-in series, a video game, and a toy line. Which media industry concept best describes this strategy?","options":{"A":"Synergy — the conglomerate uses its multiple holdings (film studio, streaming platform, games, retail) to cross-promote a single IP, maximising revenue while each arm markets the others.","B":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","C":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-029","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Public service broadcasting (PSB) obligations in the UK require broadcasters to:","options":{"A":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","B":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","C":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","D":"Serve the public interest by providing informative, educational and culturally diverse content — including programmes that would not be commercially viable — and ensuring universal access regardless of ability to pay."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"PSB in the UK: BBC (licence fee funded), ITV/Channel 4/Channel 5 (commercial but with PSB obligations). Core PSB requirements: universality of access; range and diversity of content; information, education and entertainment; regional programming; content that would be commercially unviable (minority languages, disability programming, arts). Regulatory body: Ofcom. BBC Charter sets obligations. Debates: is PSB still relevant in a streaming era? Netflix/Amazon/YouTube provide choice without PSB obligations. Charter renewal debates (BBC 2027): should licence fee model continue? Criticism from the right: BBC has political bias. Criticism from the left: cuts threaten diverse programming.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A Channel 4 documentary about life in a deprived Yorkshire town receives low ratings but wins a BAFTA and is praised for giving voice to underrepresented communities. This is best understood as:","options":{"A":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","B":"Channel 4 fulfilling its PSB remit — producing content of cultural value that serves underrepresented audiences and would be unlikely to be made by a purely commercial broadcaster focused solely on ratings and profit.","C":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","D":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-031","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The 'two-step flow' model of media influence (Katz and Lazarsfeld) argues that:","options":{"A":"Media messages reach audiences primarily through opinion leaders — influential individuals who consume media, interpret it, and then pass on filtered versions to their social networks — rather than flowing directly and uniformly to a passive mass audience.","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","D":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Two-step flow (Katz & Lazarsfeld, 1955 — 'Personal Influence'): challenged hypodermic needle model using empirical research on voting behaviour and fashion. Step 1: opinion leaders (politically engaged, media-heavy consumers) receive and interpret media messages. Step 2: opinion leaders pass interpreted messages to their social networks through conversation, recommendation and discussion. Implications: (1) social relationships mediate media effects; (2) audiences are not isolated or uniform; (3) personal influence is often more persuasive than direct media. Modern relevance: social media influencers operate as digital opinion leaders — the model maps well onto Instagram/TikTok recommendation culture. Limitation: oversimplifies; ignores multi-directional flows in social media era.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A beauty influencer with 2 million Instagram followers reviews a new skincare product. Their followers buy it in large numbers, despite no traditional advertising. Which media concept best explains this?","options":{"A":"The hypodermic model","B":"Production budget","C":"Two-step flow — the influencer acts as an opinion leader, having consumed and evaluated the product and then passing a filtered, personalised recommendation to their social network, whose trust in the influencer makes the message more persuasive than a direct advertisement.","D":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-033","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Ofcom's duty to maintain 'due impartiality' in broadcast news requires that:","options":{"A":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","B":"Broadcasters must present controversial matters with sufficient balance — not favouring any particular viewpoint — though this does not mean every perspective receives identical airtime, but that no significant strand of opinion is unreasonably excluded or misrepresented.","C":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","D":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Due impartiality (Ofcom Broadcasting Code Section 5): applies to ALL broadcasters (not print — IPSO). Not equal airtime but 'adequate' balance over time. Distinction: news (impartiality strictly required) vs. factual programmes vs. comment/opinion. Broadcast vs press: press (The Sun, Guardian) can be politically partisan — not regulated for impartiality. Broadcast must be impartial because: public ownership of airwaves; scarcity of broadcast spectrum historically (though this is less relevant in digital age). Debates: does social media undermine the concept of regulated impartiality? Foreign news channels (Russia Today, Al Jazeera) and UK regulatory jurisdiction. Exam: compare broadcast regulation with press self-regulation (IPSO) — very different frameworks.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"The Sun newspaper endorses a political party the day before a general election. A television news channel does the same. Which of these actions would breach UK media regulation?","options":{"A":"Vertical editing","B":"a restriction on digital distribution","C":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","D":"Only the television news channel — broadcast media are regulated by Ofcom's due impartiality rules, which prohibit political endorsements. The Sun, as a print publication, is not subject to broadcast impartiality requirements and may express political opinions freely."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-035","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"cultivation theory suggests that heavy exposure to media may:","options":{"A":"Reception theory","B":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","C":"shape perceptions of social reality over time","D":"Regulatory code"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Gerbner's theory links repeated patterns of representation with worldviews.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"cultivation theory suggests that heavy exposure to media may:","options":{"A":"shape perceptions of social reality over time","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"Two-step flow","D":"Production budget"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-037","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A niche audience is:","options":{"A":"Global conglomerate","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"A preferred reading","D":"a relatively small, specialised audience group"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Niche products can serve specialised interests that mass media overlook.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A niche audience is:","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"a relatively small, specialised audience group","C":"Primary institution","D":"Ownership structure"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-039","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media rating is intended to measure:","options":{"A":"the size or composition of an audience","B":"Primary institution","C":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Ratings support scheduling, advertising and commissioning decisions.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media rating is intended to measure:","options":{"A":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","B":"A production code","C":"the size or composition of an audience","D":"Global conglomerate"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-041","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Livingstone and Lunt argue that digital media regulation faces:","options":{"A":"Vertical editing","B":"a gap between powerful global platforms and national regulation","C":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","D":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Global online services complicate accountability and consumer protection.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Livingstone and Lunt argue that digital media regulation faces:","options":{"A":"no change from print regulation","B":"Cultivation — US audiences have been cultivated to prefer domestic content.","C":"only technical problems with cameras","D":"a gap between powerful global platforms and national regulation"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-043","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The male gaze describes:","options":{"A":"Distribution channel","B":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","C":"representation that positions women as objects of a heterosexual male look","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Mulvey links visual pleasure and narrative perspective to gendered power.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"The male gaze describes:","options":{"A":"representation that positions women as objects of a heterosexual male look","B":"Cultivation only","C":"Genre convention","D":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-045","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Globalisation can benefit media industries by:","options":{"A":"Rating","B":"Local regulation only","C":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","D":"opening access to larger markets and investment"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Global markets create opportunities but can also intensify cultural and economic inequalities.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Globalisation can benefit media industries by:","options":{"A":"Gatekeeping","B":"opening access to larger markets and investment","C":"Audience isolation","D":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-047","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"User-generated content is produced by:","options":{"A":"ordinary users rather than solely professional institutions","B":"Rating measures ideology","C":"Institutional ownership","D":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"UGC can widen participation while raising issues of accuracy, moderation and exploitation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"User-generated content is produced by:","options":{"A":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","B":"User-generated producer","C":"ordinary users rather than solely professional institutions","D":"Genre removes choice"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-049","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A regulation code is intended to:","options":{"A":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","B":"set standards and responsibilities for media practice","C":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","D":"a restriction on digital distribution"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Regulation may address harm, privacy, accuracy, fairness and access.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A regulation code is intended to:","options":{"A":"guarantee a programme is profitable","B":"Vertical editing","C":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","D":"set standards and responsibilities for media practice"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-051","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Stuart Hall's encoding/decoding model argues that audiences: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","B":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","C":"Actively decode media texts, and the meaning created depends on the viewer's cultural background — producers encode a preferred reading, but audiences may adopt a negotiated or oppositional reading.","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Hall (1980) encoding/decoding: producers encode a preferred reading using dominant ideology. Audiences decode from their own cultural position: (1) Dominant/hegemonic — accepts preferred meaning fully; (2) Negotiated — partially accepts, modifies from own position; (3) Oppositional — rejects preferred reading, substitutes their own. Key point: meaning is not fixed in the text but created in the interaction between text and reader. Exam: apply this when analysing audience response, not just representation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A news broadcast frames immigration negatively. A viewer who is themselves an immigrant rejects this framing and sees the report as biased. Hall would describe this as:","options":{"A":"An oppositional reading — the viewer's social position (as an immigrant) leads them to reject the preferred meaning and substitute an alternative interpretation based on their own experience.","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"Production budget","D":"Reception theory"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-053","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Gerbner's cultivation theory argues that: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","C":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","D":"Long-term, heavy exposure to television cultivates a shared worldview — heavy viewers develop a distorted perception of social reality that more closely matches the TV world than actual social statistics, particularly regarding crime and danger (the 'mean world syndrome')."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Gerbner (1970s+) cultivation theory: TV is the dominant storytelling medium. Heavy viewers (4+ hours/day) develop TV-cultivated perceptions: overestimate crime rates, police violence prevalence, and physical danger. 'Mean world syndrome' — heavy viewers more fearful, more conservative about crime. First-order effects (facts) and second-order effects (values/attitudes). Mainstreaming — heavy viewing homogenises diverse groups toward TV's dominant values. Criticisms: correlation not causation; people choose programmes (reinforcement not cultivation); ignores active audience; pre-digital TV environment.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Research finds heavy TV viewers consistently overestimate the percentage of the population who are criminals. Gerbner would explain this as:","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"Cultivation — heavy TV exposure leads viewers to perceive social reality as matching TV's overrepresentation of crime, producing first-order cultivation effects on factual beliefs about the world.","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"Production budget"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-055","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Livingstone and Lunt's critique of regulation argues that: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"Traditional media regulation models — designed for broadcast media with clear national gatekeepers — are inadequate for converged, digital, global media environments where audiences are also producers and national boundaries are meaningless.","B":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","C":"a restriction on digital distribution","D":"Vertical editing"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Livingstone & Lunt (2011): traditional regulation framework built on broadcast model (few producers, many passive consumers, national transmission). Digital convergence undermines this: (1) Audiences become producers (UGC — YouTube, TikTok); (2) Global platforms (Netflix, Google) operate across jurisdictions; (3) Algorithmic curation not 'editorial' in traditional sense; (4) Speed of content creation outpaces regulatory response. Regulatory bodies: Ofcom (UK broadcasting + telecoms), IPSO (press), BBFC (film classification), ASA (advertising). OSA 2023 — new attempt to regulate online harms. Exam: evaluate whether any regulatory model can address these challenges.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A teenager is exposed to harmful content via a recommendation algorithm on a social media platform. This situation most clearly illustrates:","options":{"A":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","B":"Broadcasters must present controversial matters with sufficient balance — not favouring any particular viewpoint — though this does not mean every perspective receives identical airtime, but that no significant strand of opinion is unreasonably excluded or misrepresented.","C":"Livingstone and Lunt's argument that traditional national regulation cannot govern global digital platforms operating across jurisdictions.","D":"Cultivation theory — long-term algorithmic exposure shapes users' worldview."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-057","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The 'hypodermic needle' model of media effects assumes: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The hypodermic model","B":"Media messages are injected directly into passive audiences who absorb them uncritically, producing immediate, uniform behavioural effects — a simple, direct cause-and-effect relationship.","C":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","D":"Production budget"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Hypodermic needle (magic bullet) model: early effects theory (1920s-40s). Assumes: audiences are passive, homogeneous, isolated from social ties; media has direct, powerful, immediate effects. Associated with: Bandura's Bobo doll experiments (children imitated modelled aggression); moral panics about film violence. Criticisms: (1) audiences are NOT passive — two-step flow (Katz & Lazarsfeld), active audience theory; (2) social context mediates effects; (3) laboratory conditions are artificial; (4) correlation ≠ causation. Modern relevance: still cited in political debates (video games/violence) despite weak evidence. Exam: most useful as a model to critique rather than apply.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Research attempting to prove that violent video games cause violent behaviour repeatedly fails to establish a causal link. This most directly undermines:","options":{"A":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","B":"Reception theory","C":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","D":"The hypodermic needle model — which predicts direct, measurable behavioural effects from media exposure. The absence of clear causal evidence suggests audiences are not the passive receptacles this model assumes."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-059","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"in semiotic analysis, a sign is: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"Ownership pattern","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"something that communicates a meaning","D":"A rating"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Signs include words, images, sounds and conventions that audiences interpret.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"in semiotic analysis, a sign is:","options":{"A":"something that communicates a meaning","B":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","C":"A mode of address","D":"a production budget"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-061","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Denotation refers to: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"Institutional ownership","B":"Ownership pattern","C":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","D":"the literal or descriptive meaning of a media sign"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Denotation is distinguished from connotation, which concerns associated meanings.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Denotation refers to:","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"the literal or descriptive meaning of a media sign","C":"A mode of address","D":"A rating"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-063","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Hall's representation theory argues that media representations: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"construct meaning rather than simply mirror reality","B":"The hypodermic model","C":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","D":"are created only by audiences"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Representation selects, frames and organises meanings through codes and conventions.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Hall's representation theory argues that media representations:","options":{"A":"Distribution channel","B":"Genre convention","C":"construct meaning rather than simply mirror reality","D":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-065","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media genre is useful because it: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Naturalistic underexposure","B":"sets recognisable conventions and expectations","C":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","D":"removes the need for marketing"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Genres help producers and audiences organise expectations, while texts can subvert them.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media genre is useful because it:","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"A public service announcement","C":"removes the need for marketing","D":"sets recognisable conventions and expectations"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-067","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A close-up is often used to emphasise: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"Naturalistic underexposure","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"facial expression or emotional detail","D":"removes the need for marketing"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Shot size directs attention and shapes audience involvement.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A close-up is often used to emphasise:","options":{"A":"facial expression or emotional detail","B":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","C":"Oppositional reading","D":"A public service announcement"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-069","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media institution is: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","B":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","C":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","D":"an organisation involved in producing, distributing or regulating media"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Institutions operate within economic, technological and regulatory contexts.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media institution is:","options":{"A":"The process by which audiences become more homogeneous in their media consumption habits.","B":"an organisation involved in producing, distributing or regulating media","C":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","D":"Horizontal audience fragmentation"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-071","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A public service broadcaster is expected to: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"serve wider public purposes rather than only maximise profit","B":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","C":"Public service broadcasting","D":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"PSB obligations can include range, quality, diversity and access.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A public service broadcaster is expected to:","options":{"A":"only two unrelated products","B":"broadcast only to subscribers","C":"serve wider public purposes rather than only maximise profit","D":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-073","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Media convergence describes: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","B":"the coming together of technologies, industries or media forms","C":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","D":"a restriction on digital distribution"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Convergence can change production, circulation and consumption.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Media convergence describes:","options":{"A":"Self-regulation by media companies always protects the public interest.","B":"Vertical editing","C":"a restriction on digital distribution","D":"the coming together of technologies, industries or media forms"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-075","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"An audience demographic is based on characteristics such as: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"Primary institution","C":"age, gender, income or education","D":"A production code"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Demographics help institutions identify and sell to audience groups.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"An audience demographic is based on characteristics such as:","options":{"A":"age, gender, income or education","B":"a restriction on digital distribution","C":"A preferred reading","D":"A stereotype"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-077","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The concept of 'synergy' in the media industry refers to: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","B":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","C":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","D":"The mutual promotion of products across a conglomerate's holdings — where one product markets another across different media platforms, multiplying revenue from a single IP."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Synergy: when a conglomerate's various arms reinforce each other commercially. Example: Marvel Cinematic Universe — Disney produces the film (studio), releases on Disney+ (streaming), sells merchandise through Disney stores, promotes via ABC News (TV), covers in Marvel comics (publishing). Each outlet promotes the others — the whole is worth more than the sum of its parts. This is why conglomerates acquire across media types, not just depth. Exam link: synergy benefits large conglomerates, making it harder for independent producers to compete — connects to Curran & Seaton's diversity concerns.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Disney releases a new superhero film, simultaneously launching a Disney+ tie-in series, a video game, and a toy line. Which media industry concept best describes this strategy?","options":{"A":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","B":"Synergy — the conglomerate uses its multiple holdings (film studio, streaming platform, games, retail) to cross-promote a single IP, maximising revenue while each arm markets the others.","C":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-079","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Public service broadcasting (PSB) obligations in the UK require broadcasters to: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"Serve the public interest by providing informative, educational and culturally diverse content — including programmes that would not be commercially viable — and ensuring universal access regardless of ability to pay.","B":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","C":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","D":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"PSB in the UK: BBC (licence fee funded), ITV/Channel 4/Channel 5 (commercial but with PSB obligations). Core PSB requirements: universality of access; range and diversity of content; information, education and entertainment; regional programming; content that would be commercially unviable (minority languages, disability programming, arts). Regulatory body: Ofcom. BBC Charter sets obligations. Debates: is PSB still relevant in a streaming era? Netflix/Amazon/YouTube provide choice without PSB obligations. Charter renewal debates (BBC 2027): should licence fee model continue? Criticism from the right: BBC has political bias. Criticism from the left: cuts threaten diverse programming.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A Channel 4 documentary about life in a deprived Yorkshire town receives low ratings but wins a BAFTA and is praised for giving voice to underrepresented communities. This is best understood as:","options":{"A":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","B":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","C":"Channel 4 fulfilling its PSB remit — producing content of cultural value that serves underrepresented audiences and would be unlikely to be made by a purely commercial broadcaster focused solely on ratings and profit.","D":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-081","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The 'two-step flow' model of media influence (Katz and Lazarsfeld) argues that: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","B":"Media messages reach audiences primarily through opinion leaders — influential individuals who consume media, interpret it, and then pass on filtered versions to their social networks — rather than flowing directly and uniformly to a passive mass audience.","C":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Two-step flow (Katz & Lazarsfeld, 1955 — 'Personal Influence'): challenged hypodermic needle model using empirical research on voting behaviour and fashion. Step 1: opinion leaders (politically engaged, media-heavy consumers) receive and interpret media messages. Step 2: opinion leaders pass interpreted messages to their social networks through conversation, recommendation and discussion. Implications: (1) social relationships mediate media effects; (2) audiences are not isolated or uniform; (3) personal influence is often more persuasive than direct media. Modern relevance: social media influencers operate as digital opinion leaders — the model maps well onto Instagram/TikTok recommendation culture. Limitation: oversimplifies; ignores multi-directional flows in social media era.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A beauty influencer with 2 million Instagram followers reviews a new skincare product. Their followers buy it in large numbers, despite no traditional advertising. Which media concept best explains this?","options":{"A":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","B":"Production budget","C":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","D":"Two-step flow — the influencer acts as an opinion leader, having consumed and evaluated the product and then passing a filtered, personalised recommendation to their social network, whose trust in the influencer makes the message more persuasive than a direct advertisement."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-083","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"ofcom's duty to maintain 'due impartiality' in broadcast news requires that: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","B":"a restriction on digital distribution","C":"Broadcasters must present controversial matters with sufficient balance — not favouring any particular viewpoint — though this does not mean every perspective receives identical airtime, but that no significant strand of opinion is unreasonably excluded or misrepresented.","D":"Vertical editing"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Due impartiality (Ofcom Broadcasting Code Section 5): applies to ALL broadcasters (not print — IPSO). Not equal airtime but 'adequate' balance over time. Distinction: news (impartiality strictly required) vs. factual programmes vs. comment/opinion. Broadcast vs press: press (The Sun, Guardian) can be politically partisan — not regulated for impartiality. Broadcast must be impartial because: public ownership of airwaves; scarcity of broadcast spectrum historically (though this is less relevant in digital age). Debates: does social media undermine the concept of regulated impartiality? Foreign news channels (Russia Today, Al Jazeera) and UK regulatory jurisdiction. Exam: compare broadcast regulation with press self-regulation (IPSO) — very different frameworks.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"The Sun newspaper endorses a political party the day before a general election. A television news channel does the same. Which of these actions would breach UK media regulation?","options":{"A":"Only the television news channel — broadcast media are regulated by Ofcom's due impartiality rules, which prohibit political endorsements. The Sun, as a print publication, is not subject to broadcast impartiality requirements and may express political opinions freely.","B":"Media regulation is unnecessary in a digital age.","C":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","D":"Broadcasters must present controversial matters with sufficient balance — not favouring any particular viewpoint — though this does not mean every perspective receives identical airtime, but that no significant strand of opinion is unreasonably excluded or misrepresented."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-085","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Cultivation theory suggests that heavy exposure to media may: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"Regulatory code","B":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","C":"Two-step flow","D":"shape perceptions of social reality over time"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Gerbner's theory links repeated patterns of representation with worldviews.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Cultivation theory suggests that heavy exposure to media may:","options":{"A":"Reception theory","B":"shape perceptions of social reality over time","C":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","D":"Production budget"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-087","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A niche audience is: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"a relatively small, specialised audience group","B":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","C":"A production code","D":"Primary institution"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Niche products can serve specialised interests that mass media overlook.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A niche audience is:","options":{"A":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","B":"A preferred reading","C":"a relatively small, specialised audience group","D":"Ownership structure"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-089","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media rating is intended to measure: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"the size or composition of an audience","C":"Ownership structure","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Ratings support scheduling, advertising and commissioning decisions.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media rating is intended to measure:","options":{"A":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","B":"A production code","C":"Primary institution","D":"the size or composition of an audience"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-091","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Livingstone and Lunt argue that digital media regulation faces: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"no change from print regulation","B":"Vertical editing","C":"a gap between powerful global platforms and national regulation","D":"Cultivation — US audiences have been cultivated to prefer domestic content."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Global online services complicate accountability and consumer protection.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Livingstone and Lunt argue that digital media regulation faces:","options":{"A":"a gap between powerful global platforms and national regulation","B":"a restriction on digital distribution","C":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","D":"only technical problems with cameras"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-093","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The male gaze describes: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The hypodermic model","B":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","C":"Genre convention","D":"representation that positions women as objects of a heterosexual male look"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Mulvey links visual pleasure and narrative perspective to gendered power.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"The male gaze describes:","options":{"A":"Distribution channel","B":"representation that positions women as objects of a heterosexual male look","C":"Cultivation only","D":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-095","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Globalisation can benefit media industries by: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"opening access to larger markets and investment","B":"Local regulation only","C":"Gatekeeping","D":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Global markets create opportunities but can also intensify cultural and economic inequalities.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Globalisation can benefit media industries by:","options":{"A":"Audience isolation","B":"Rating","C":"opening access to larger markets and investment","D":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-097","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"User-generated content is produced by: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"A preferred reading","B":"ordinary users rather than solely professional institutions","C":"Institutional ownership","D":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"UGC can widen participation while raising issues of accuracy, moderation and exploitation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"User-generated content is produced by:","options":{"A":"User-generated producer","B":"Genre removes choice","C":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","D":"ordinary users rather than solely professional institutions"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-099","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A regulation code is intended to: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"guarantee a programme is profitable","B":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","C":"set standards and responsibilities for media practice","D":"a restriction on digital distribution"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Regulation may address harm, privacy, accuracy, fairness and access.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A regulation code is intended to:","options":{"A":"set standards and responsibilities for media practice","B":"Vertical editing","C":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","D":"prevent every form of representation"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-101","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Stuart Hall's encoding/decoding model argues that audiences: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","C":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","D":"Actively decode media texts, and the meaning created depends on the viewer's cultural background — producers encode a preferred reading, but audiences may adopt a negotiated or oppositional reading."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Hall (1980) encoding/decoding: producers encode a preferred reading using dominant ideology. Audiences decode from their own cultural position: (1) Dominant/hegemonic — accepts preferred meaning fully; (2) Negotiated — partially accepts, modifies from own position; (3) Oppositional — rejects preferred reading, substitutes their own. Key point: meaning is not fixed in the text but created in the interaction between text and reader. Exam: apply this when analysing audience response, not just representation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which option is defensible when the context changes? A news broadcast frames immigration negatively. A viewer who is themselves an immigrant rejects this framing and sees the report as biased. Hall would describe this as:","options":{"A":"Production budget","B":"An oppositional reading — the viewer's social position (as an immigrant) leads them to reject the preferred meaning and substitute an alternative interpretation based on their own experience.","C":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","D":"Reception theory"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-103","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Gerbner's cultivation theory argues that: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"Long-term, heavy exposure to television cultivates a shared worldview — heavy viewers develop a distorted perception of social reality that more closely matches the TV world than actual social statistics, particularly regarding crime and danger (the 'mean world syndrome').","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","D":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Gerbner (1970s+) cultivation theory: TV is the dominant storytelling medium. Heavy viewers (4+ hours/day) develop TV-cultivated perceptions: overestimate crime rates, police violence prevalence, and physical danger. 'Mean world syndrome' — heavy viewers more fearful, more conservative about crime. First-order effects (facts) and second-order effects (values/attitudes). Mainstreaming — heavy viewing homogenises diverse groups toward TV's dominant values. Criticisms: correlation not causation; people choose programmes (reinforcement not cultivation); ignores active audience; pre-digital TV environment.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Research finds heavy TV viewers consistently overestimate the percentage of the population who are criminals. Gerbner would explain this as:","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"The hypodermic model","C":"Cultivation — heavy TV exposure leads viewers to perceive social reality as matching TV's overrepresentation of crime, producing first-order cultivation effects on factual beliefs about the world.","D":"Production budget"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-105","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"livingstone and Lunt's critique of regulation argues that: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"Traditional media regulation models — designed for broadcast media with clear national gatekeepers — are inadequate for converged, digital, global media environments where audiences are also producers and national boundaries are meaningless.","C":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","D":"a restriction on digital distribution"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Livingstone & Lunt (2011): traditional regulation framework built on broadcast model (few producers, many passive consumers, national transmission). Digital convergence undermines this: (1) Audiences become producers (UGC — YouTube, TikTok); (2) Global platforms (Netflix, Google) operate across jurisdictions; (3) Algorithmic curation not 'editorial' in traditional sense; (4) Speed of content creation outpaces regulatory response. Regulatory bodies: Ofcom (UK broadcasting + telecoms), IPSO (press), BBFC (film classification), ASA (advertising). OSA 2023 — new attempt to regulate online harms. Exam: evaluate whether any regulatory model can address these challenges.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A teenager is exposed to harmful content via a recommendation algorithm on a social media platform. This situation most clearly illustrates:","options":{"A":"Vertical editing","B":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","C":"Cultivation theory — long-term algorithmic exposure shapes users' worldview.","D":"Livingstone and Lunt's argument that traditional national regulation cannot govern global digital platforms operating across jurisdictions."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-107","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The 'hypodermic needle' model of media effects assumes: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","C":"Media messages are injected directly into passive audiences who absorb them uncritically, producing immediate, uniform behavioural effects — a simple, direct cause-and-effect relationship.","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Hypodermic needle (magic bullet) model: early effects theory (1920s-40s). Assumes: audiences are passive, homogeneous, isolated from social ties; media has direct, powerful, immediate effects. Associated with: Bandura's Bobo doll experiments (children imitated modelled aggression); moral panics about film violence. Criticisms: (1) audiences are NOT passive — two-step flow (Katz & Lazarsfeld), active audience theory; (2) social context mediates effects; (3) laboratory conditions are artificial; (4) correlation ≠ causation. Modern relevance: still cited in political debates (video games/violence) despite weak evidence. Exam: most useful as a model to critique rather than apply.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Research attempting to prove that violent video games cause violent behaviour repeatedly fails to establish a causal link. This most directly undermines:","options":{"A":"The hypodermic needle model — which predicts direct, measurable behavioural effects from media exposure. The absence of clear causal evidence suggests audiences are not the passive receptacles this model assumes.","B":"Production budget","C":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","D":"Reception theory"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-109","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"In semiotic analysis, a sign is: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"A mode of address","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"Institutional ownership","D":"something that communicates a meaning"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Signs include words, images, sounds and conventions that audiences interpret.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A red heart icon is used to represent love in an advert. In semiotics, the icon is a:","options":{"A":"Sign","B":"Ownership pattern","C":"A rating","D":"Demographic"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-111","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Denotation refers to: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"the literal or descriptive meaning of a media sign","B":"Institutional ownership","C":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","D":"A mode of address"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Denotation is distinguished from connotation, which concerns associated meanings.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"When analysing an image, a student lists the objects and colours that are literally visible before discussing meanings. This is describing:","options":{"A":"A rating","B":"Ownership pattern","C":"Denotation","D":"Vertical integration"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-113","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Hall's representation theory argues that media representations: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Distribution channel","B":"construct meaning rather than simply mirror reality","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Representation selects, frames and organises meanings through codes and conventions.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A news drama selects some groups as threatening and others as respectable. Hall's approach would say the representation is:","options":{"A":"Constructed through selection and ideology","B":"Genre convention","C":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","D":"Cultivation only"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-115","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media genre is useful because it: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"the geography of a whole setting","B":"removes the need for marketing","C":"sets recognisable conventions and expectations","D":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Genres help producers and audiences organise expectations, while texts can subvert them.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A streaming platform groups crime dramas together so audiences know what style and conventions to expect. This illustrates the usefulness of:","options":{"A":"A public service announcement","B":"Naturalistic underexposure","C":"Genre","D":"Oppositional reading"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-117","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A close-up is often used to emphasise: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"the geography of a whole setting","B":"A public service announcement","C":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","D":"facial expression or emotional detail"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Shot size directs attention and shapes audience involvement.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A film cuts tightly to a character's eyes as they hear unexpected news. What is the main effect of this shot?","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"It emphasises emotion and reaction","C":"Naturalistic underexposure","D":"Oppositional reading"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-119","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media institution is: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"an organisation involved in producing, distributing or regulating media","B":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","C":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","D":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Institutions operate within economic, technological and regulatory contexts.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A company that funds, produces and distributes television programmes is a media:","options":{"A":"The process by which audiences become more homogeneous in their media consumption habits.","B":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","C":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","D":"Institution"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-121","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A public service broadcaster is expected to: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","B":"serve wider public purposes rather than only maximise profit","C":"Horizontal audience fragmentation","D":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"PSB obligations can include range, quality, diversity and access.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A broadcaster is required to provide news, education and content for minority audiences rather than only chase profit. This reflects a:","options":{"A":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","B":"Public service remit","C":"The process by which audiences become more homogeneous in their media consumption habits.","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-123","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Media convergence describes: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"a restriction on digital distribution","B":"Vertical editing","C":"the coming together of technologies, industries or media forms","D":"Self-regulation by media companies always protects the public interest."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Convergence can change production, circulation and consumption.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A smartphone combines filming, editing, publishing and social interaction in one device. This is media:","options":{"A":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","B":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","C":"Linear narration","D":"Convergence"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-125","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"An audience demographic is based on characteristics such as: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"Global conglomerate","C":"Primary institution","D":"age, gender, income or education"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Demographics help institutions identify and sell to audience groups.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"An audience profile records viewers' age, gender, income and location. These are examples of:","options":{"A":"media products cannot be targeted","B":"A production code","C":"Demographic characteristics","D":"A stereotype"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-127","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The concept of 'synergy' in the media industry refers to: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The mutual promotion of products across a conglomerate's holdings — where one product markets another across different media platforms, multiplying revenue from a single IP.","B":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","C":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","D":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Synergy: when a conglomerate's various arms reinforce each other commercially. Example: Marvel Cinematic Universe — Disney produces the film (studio), releases on Disney+ (streaming), sells merchandise through Disney stores, promotes via ABC News (TV), covers in Marvel comics (publishing). Each outlet promotes the others — the whole is worth more than the sum of its parts. This is why conglomerates acquire across media types, not just depth. Exam link: synergy benefits large conglomerates, making it harder for independent producers to compete — connects to Curran & Seaton's diversity concerns.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Disney releases a new superhero film, simultaneously launching a Disney+ tie-in series, a video game, and a toy line. Which media industry concept best describes this strategy?","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","C":"Synergy — the conglomerate uses its multiple holdings (film studio, streaming platform, games, retail) to cross-promote a single IP, maximising revenue while each arm markets the others.","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-129","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"public service broadcasting (PSB) obligations in the UK require broadcasters to: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","B":"Serve the public interest by providing informative, educational and culturally diverse content — including programmes that would not be commercially viable — and ensuring universal access regardless of ability to pay.","C":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","D":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"PSB in the UK: BBC (licence fee funded), ITV/Channel 4/Channel 5 (commercial but with PSB obligations). Core PSB requirements: universality of access; range and diversity of content; information, education and entertainment; regional programming; content that would be commercially unviable (minority languages, disability programming, arts). Regulatory body: Ofcom. BBC Charter sets obligations. Debates: is PSB still relevant in a streaming era? Netflix/Amazon/YouTube provide choice without PSB obligations. Charter renewal debates (BBC 2027): should licence fee model continue? Criticism from the right: BBC has political bias. Criticism from the left: cuts threaten diverse programming.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A Channel 4 documentary about life in a deprived Yorkshire town receives low ratings but wins a BAFTA and is praised for giving voice to underrepresented communities. This is best understood as:","options":{"A":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","B":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","C":"The process by which audiences become more homogeneous in their media consumption habits.","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling its PSB remit — producing content of cultural value that serves underrepresented audiences and would be unlikely to be made by a purely commercial broadcaster focused solely on ratings and profit."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-131","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The 'two-step flow' model of media influence (Katz and Lazarsfeld) argues that: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"Media messages reach audiences primarily through opinion leaders — influential individuals who consume media, interpret it, and then pass on filtered versions to their social networks — rather than flowing directly and uniformly to a passive mass audience.","D":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Two-step flow (Katz & Lazarsfeld, 1955 — 'Personal Influence'): challenged hypodermic needle model using empirical research on voting behaviour and fashion. Step 1: opinion leaders (politically engaged, media-heavy consumers) receive and interpret media messages. Step 2: opinion leaders pass interpreted messages to their social networks through conversation, recommendation and discussion. Implications: (1) social relationships mediate media effects; (2) audiences are not isolated or uniform; (3) personal influence is often more persuasive than direct media. Modern relevance: social media influencers operate as digital opinion leaders — the model maps well onto Instagram/TikTok recommendation culture. Limitation: oversimplifies; ignores multi-directional flows in social media era.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A beauty influencer with 2 million Instagram followers reviews a new skincare product. Their followers buy it in large numbers, despite no traditional advertising. Which media concept best explains this?","options":{"A":"Two-step flow — the influencer acts as an opinion leader, having consumed and evaluated the product and then passing a filtered, personalised recommendation to their social network, whose trust in the influencer makes the message more persuasive than a direct advertisement.","B":"Production budget","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-133","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Ofcom's duty to maintain 'due impartiality' in broadcast news requires that: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","D":"Broadcasters must present controversial matters with sufficient balance — not favouring any particular viewpoint — though this does not mean every perspective receives identical airtime, but that no significant strand of opinion is unreasonably excluded or misrepresented."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Due impartiality (Ofcom Broadcasting Code Section 5): applies to ALL broadcasters (not print — IPSO). Not equal airtime but 'adequate' balance over time. Distinction: news (impartiality strictly required) vs. factual programmes vs. comment/opinion. Broadcast vs press: press (The Sun, Guardian) can be politically partisan — not regulated for impartiality. Broadcast must be impartial because: public ownership of airwaves; scarcity of broadcast spectrum historically (though this is less relevant in digital age). Debates: does social media undermine the concept of regulated impartiality? Foreign news channels (Russia Today, Al Jazeera) and UK regulatory jurisdiction. Exam: compare broadcast regulation with press self-regulation (IPSO) — very different frameworks.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"The Sun newspaper endorses a political party the day before a general election. A television news channel does the same. Which of these actions would breach UK media regulation?","options":{"A":"Vertical editing","B":"Only the television news channel — broadcast media are regulated by Ofcom's due impartiality rules, which prohibit political endorsements. The Sun, as a print publication, is not subject to broadcast impartiality requirements and may express political opinions freely.","C":"a restriction on digital distribution","D":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-135","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Cultivation theory suggests that heavy exposure to media may: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"shape perceptions of social reality over time","B":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","C":"Reception theory","D":"Regulatory code"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Gerbner's theory links repeated patterns of representation with worldviews.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Cultivation theory suggests that heavy exposure to media may:","options":{"A":"Two-step flow","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"shape perceptions of social reality over time","D":"Production budget"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-137","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A niche audience is: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"a relatively small, specialised audience group","C":"Ownership structure","D":"Primary institution"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Niche products can serve specialised interests that mass media overlook.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A niche audience is:","options":{"A":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","B":"A preferred reading","C":"A production code","D":"a relatively small, specialised audience group"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-139","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A media rating is intended to measure: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"Primary institution","C":"the size or composition of an audience","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Ratings support scheduling, advertising and commissioning decisions.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media rating is intended to measure:","options":{"A":"the size or composition of an audience","B":"A production code","C":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","D":"Global conglomerate"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-141","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Livingstone and Lunt argue that digital media regulation faces: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","B":"Vertical editing","C":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","D":"a gap between powerful global platforms and national regulation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Global online services complicate accountability and consumer protection.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Livingstone and Lunt argue that digital media regulation faces:","options":{"A":"no change from print regulation","B":"a gap between powerful global platforms and national regulation","C":"Cultivation — US audiences have been cultivated to prefer domestic content.","D":"only technical problems with cameras"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-143","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"The male gaze describes: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"representation that positions women as objects of a heterosexual male look","B":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","C":"Distribution channel","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Mulvey links visual pleasure and narrative perspective to gendered power.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"The male gaze describes:","options":{"A":"Genre convention","B":"Cultivation only","C":"representation that positions women as objects of a heterosexual male look","D":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-145","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"Globalisation can benefit media industries by: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Local regulation only","B":"opening access to larger markets and investment","C":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","D":"Rating"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Global markets create opportunities but can also intensify cultural and economic inequalities.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Globalisation can benefit media industries by:","options":{"A":"Gatekeeping","B":"Audience isolation","C":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","D":"opening access to larger markets and investment"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-147","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"User-generated content is produced by: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"Genre removes choice","B":"Institutional ownership","C":"ordinary users rather than solely professional institutions","D":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"UGC can widen participation while raising issues of accuracy, moderation and exploitation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"User-generated content is produced by:","options":{"A":"ordinary users rather than solely professional institutions","B":"User-generated producer","C":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-149","spec":"MEDIA-1","stem":"A regulation code is intended to: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"a restriction on digital distribution","B":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","C":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","D":"set standards and responsibilities for media practice"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Regulation may address harm, privacy, accuracy, fairness and access.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A regulation code is intended to:","options":{"A":"guarantee a programme is profitable","B":"set standards and responsibilities for media practice","C":"Vertical editing","D":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"}]},"MEDIA-2":{"label":"Industry & Audience","color":"#7c2d12","questions":[{"id":"MED-09","stem":"In media industry terms, 'vertical integration' means:","options":{"A":"A media company owning businesses at multiple stages of the same production and distribution chain — for example, a studio that produces films, owns the distribution network and operates the cinemas that screen them.","B":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","C":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","D":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Vertical integration: ownership across different stages of one supply chain (production → distribution → exhibition). Example: Disney produces content (studio), distributes it (Disney+, theatrical), and licenses merchandise — controlling the whole chain. Benefits: cost efficiencies, control over release windows and pricing, guaranteed outlets. Risks: regulatory scrutiny (anti-competition). Contrast with horizontal integration: buying competitors at the SAME level (Disney acquiring 21st Century Fox — both studios). Exam: always specify which type and why it matters for diversity/power.","reforge":{"stem":"A record label that records artists, distributes their music through its own streaming platform, and books them into venues it owns is an example of:","options":{"A":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","B":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","C":"Vertical integration — the label controls production (recording), distribution (streaming) and exhibition (venues), owning the entire supply chain from creation to audience delivery.","D":"no change from print regulation"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"MEDIA-2","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-10","stem":"The concept of 'synergy' in the media industry refers to:","options":{"A":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","B":"The mutual promotion of products across a conglomerate's holdings — where one product markets another across different media platforms, multiplying revenue from a single IP.","C":"the speed of internet access","D":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Synergy: when a conglomerate's various arms reinforce each other commercially. Example: Marvel Cinematic Universe — Disney produces the film (studio), releases on Disney+ (streaming), sells merchandise through Disney stores, promotes via ABC News (TV), covers in Marvel comics (publishing). Each outlet promotes the others — the whole is worth more than the sum of its parts. This is why conglomerates acquire across media types, not just depth. Exam link: synergy benefits large conglomerates, making it harder for independent producers to compete — connects to Curran & Seaton's diversity concerns.","reforge":{"stem":"Disney releases a new superhero film, simultaneously launching a Disney+ tie-in series, a video game, and a toy line. Which media industry concept best describes this strategy?","options":{"A":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","B":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","C":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","D":"Synergy — the conglomerate uses its multiple holdings (film studio, streaming platform, games, retail) to cross-promote a single IP, maximising revenue while each arm markets the others."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"MEDIA-2","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-11","stem":"How should the concept of 'media convergence' be understood here?","options":{"A":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","B":"The process by which audiences become more homogeneous in their media consumption habits.","C":"The merging of previously distinct media technologies, industries and content forms — so that a single device (smartphone) can deliver television, radio, newspapers, social media and interactive games, collapsing former boundaries between media types.","D":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Convergence (Jenkins): technological — devices deliver multiple media types (phone = TV + radio + newspaper + camera). Industrial — companies merge across formerly separate sectors (telecoms + media + tech: Apple, Google, Amazon all now media companies). Content — formats blend (podcast + live event + YouTube series). Cultural — audiences move fluidly between platforms for the same story (transmedia storytelling). Consequences: disrupts traditional regulatory boundaries; enables new forms of audience participation; challenges business models of legacy media (print, linear TV). Livingstone & Lunt: regulation designed for distinct sectors can't keep up with converged landscape.","reforge":{"stem":"A viewer watches a live football match on their phone, simultaneously discussing it on social media and accessing statistics via a club app. This scenario illustrates:","options":{"A":"Media convergence — a single device delivers formerly separate media experiences (television, social media, data journalism) simultaneously, and the viewer participates rather than passively consuming.","B":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","C":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","D":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"MEDIA-2","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-12","stem":"Public service broadcasting (PSB) obligations in the UK require broadcasters to:","options":{"A":"replace all audience research","B":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","C":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","D":"Serve the public interest by providing informative, educational and culturally diverse content — including programmes that would not be commercially viable — and ensuring universal access regardless of ability to pay."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"PSB in the UK: BBC (licence fee funded), ITV/Channel 4/Channel 5 (commercial but with PSB obligations). Core PSB requirements: universality of access; range and diversity of content; information, education and entertainment; regional programming; content that would be commercially unviable (minority languages, disability programming, arts). Regulatory body: Ofcom. BBC Charter sets obligations. Debates: is PSB still relevant in a streaming era? Netflix/Amazon/YouTube provide choice without PSB obligations. Charter renewal debates (BBC 2027): should licence fee model continue? Criticism from the right: BBC has political bias. Criticism from the left: cuts threaten diverse programming.","reforge":{"stem":"A Channel 4 documentary about life in a deprived Yorkshire town receives low ratings but wins a BAFTA and is praised for giving voice to underrepresented communities. This is best understood as:","options":{"A":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","B":"Channel 4 fulfilling its PSB remit — producing content of cultural value that serves underrepresented audiences and would be unlikely to be made by a purely commercial broadcaster focused solely on ratings and profit.","C":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"MEDIA-2","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-13","stem":"In Hesmondhalgh's analysis of cultural industries, the 'cultural discount' refers to:","options":{"A":"The loss of value a cultural product suffers when exported to a different cultural market","B":"Cultivation — US audiences have been cultivated to prefer domestic content.","C":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","D":"Local regulation only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Cultural discount (Hoskins & Mirus, developed by Hesmondhalgh): when media crosses cultural boundaries, local specificity reduces its value to foreign audiences. US media minimises cultural discount by: producing for large domestic market (economies of scale), using universal genres (action, romance), English language advantage, minimal cultural specificity. This gives US content a competitive advantage globally — other nations' content faces higher discount barriers. Counter-examples: Korean Wave (Hallyu) — K-drama, K-pop have reduced their cultural discount through universal genre conventions + distinctive aesthetics. Exam: connect to global media dominance debates.","reforge":{"stem":"A British sitcom with jokes about specific UK institutions (NHS, the BBC, British politics) struggles to attract audiences in the United States. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","B":"Gatekeeping","C":"The cultural discount — UK-specific references reduce the sitcom's appeal abroad","D":"Rating"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"MEDIA-2","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-14","stem":"The 'two-step flow' model of media influence (Katz and Lazarsfeld) argues that:","options":{"A":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","B":"Media messages reach audiences primarily through opinion leaders — influential individuals who consume media, interpret it, and then pass on filtered versions to their social networks — rather than flowing directly and uniformly to a passive mass audience.","C":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Two-step flow (Katz & Lazarsfeld, 1955 — 'Personal Influence'): challenged hypodermic needle model using empirical research on voting behaviour and fashion. Step 1: opinion leaders (politically engaged, media-heavy consumers) receive and interpret media messages. Step 2: opinion leaders pass interpreted messages to their social networks through conversation, recommendation and discussion. Implications: (1) social relationships mediate media effects; (2) audiences are not isolated or uniform; (3) personal influence is often more persuasive than direct media. Modern relevance: social media influencers operate as digital opinion leaders — the model maps well onto Instagram/TikTok recommendation culture. Limitation: oversimplifies; ignores multi-directional flows in social media era.","reforge":{"stem":"A beauty influencer with 2 million Instagram followers reviews a new skincare product. Their followers buy it in large numbers, despite no traditional advertising. Which media concept best explains this?","options":{"A":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","B":"Production budget","C":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","D":"Two-step flow — the influencer acts as an opinion leader, having consumed and evaluated the product and then passing a filtered, personalised recommendation to their social network, whose trust in the influencer makes the message more persuasive than a direct advertisement."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"MEDIA-2","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-15","stem":"In the context of digital media, 'user-generated content' (UGC) challenges traditional media industry models because:","options":{"A":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","B":"Stereotype controls production","C":"It blurs the distinction between producer and audience — anyone with a smartphone can create and distribute content globally, undermining the gatekeeping power of traditional media institutions and creating a competitive content environment that legacy media cannot easily replicate.","D":"Rating measures ideology"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"UGC and the 'prosumer' (Toffler): audiences as producers. Platforms enabling UGC: YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Twitch, podcasting. Challenges to legacy media: (1) Gatekeeping disrupted — publishers no longer control what reaches audiences; (2) Revenue diverted — advertising follows attention, which increasingly goes to UGC platforms; (3) Speed — UGC often breaks news faster than journalists; (4) Scale — YouTube alone uploads 500 hours of video per minute. BUT: quality control, misinformation, algorithmic curation create new problems (Livingstone & Lunt). Gauntlett's 'making is connecting' — cultural participation is democratised. Critical view: platform companies (not creators) capture most of the value; precarious 'creator economy'.","reforge":{"stem":"A teenager's home video of a local flood goes viral on TikTok, reaching 10 million viewers before any news broadcaster has covered the story. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"How UGC bypasses gatekeeping — the teenager is both producer and distributor","B":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","C":"User-generated producer","D":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"MEDIA-2","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MED-16","stem":"Ofcom's duty to maintain 'due impartiality' in broadcast news requires that:","options":{"A":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","B":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","C":"a restriction on digital distribution","D":"Broadcasters must present controversial matters with sufficient balance — not favouring any particular viewpoint — though this does not mean every perspective receives identical airtime, but that no significant strand of opinion is unreasonably excluded or misrepresented."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Due impartiality (Ofcom Broadcasting Code Section 5): applies to ALL broadcasters (not print — IPSO). Not equal airtime but 'adequate' balance over time. Distinction: news (impartiality strictly required) vs. factual programmes vs. comment/opinion. Broadcast vs press: press (The Sun, Guardian) can be politically partisan — not regulated for impartiality. Broadcast must be impartial because: public ownership of airwaves; scarcity of broadcast spectrum historically (though this is less relevant in digital age). Debates: does social media undermine the concept of regulated impartiality? Foreign news channels (Russia Today, Al Jazeera) and UK regulatory jurisdiction. Exam: compare broadcast regulation with press self-regulation (IPSO) — very different frameworks.","reforge":{"stem":"The Sun newspaper endorses a political party the day before a general election. A television news channel does the same. Which of these actions would breach UK media regulation?","options":{"A":"Vertical editing","B":"Only the television news channel — broadcast media are regulated by Ofcom's due impartiality rules, which prohibit political endorsements. The Sun, as a print publication, is not subject to broadcast impartiality requirements and may express political opinions freely.","C":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","D":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"MEDIA-2","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-01","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Uses and gratifications theory treats audiences as:","options":{"A":"active users selecting media to meet needs","B":"The hypodermic model","C":"Two-step flow","D":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Audiences may seek information, identity, social interaction or entertainment.","reforge":{"stem":"A viewer watches a programme to relax, learn and discuss it with friends. Uses and gratifications theory focuses on the audience's:","options":{"A":"Active needs and motives","B":"Reception theory","C":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","D":"Regulatory code"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-02","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Cultivation theory suggests that heavy exposure to media may:","options":{"A":"Regulatory code","B":"shape perceptions of social reality over time","C":"Cultivation — US audiences have been cultivated to prefer domestic content.","D":"Two-step flow"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Gerbner's theory links repeated patterns of representation with worldviews.","reforge":{"stem":"A researcher links heavy long-term television viewing with stronger beliefs that the world is dangerous. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"Cultivation theory","B":"The hypodermic model","C":"guarantee a programme is profitable","D":"Reception theory"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-03","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"The hypodermic needle model assumes media messages are:","options":{"A":"The hypodermic model","B":"Regulatory code","C":"directly injected into passive audiences","D":"Cultivation — US audiences have been cultivated to prefer domestic content."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"It is a simple effects model and is criticised for underestimating audience activity.","reforge":{"stem":"A campaign assumes every viewer will accept its message immediately and in the same way. Which model does this resemble?","options":{"A":"The hypodermic needle model","B":"only technical problems with cameras","C":"Reception theory","D":"Two-step flow"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-04","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A niche audience is:","options":{"A":"Ownership structure","B":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","C":"A preferred reading","D":"a relatively small, specialised audience group"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Niche products can serve specialised interests that mass media overlook.","reforge":{"stem":"A magazine is designed for a small group of specialist enthusiasts rather than a mass audience. It targets a:","options":{"A":"Niche audience","B":"Global conglomerate","C":"A stereotype","D":"Primary institution"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-05","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Audience fragmentation means:","options":{"A":"audiences are divided across more channels and platforms","B":"Global conglomerate","C":"Primary institution","D":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Digital choice can make audiences more dispersed and specialised.","reforge":{"stem":"Streaming services give different groups highly specialised content, reducing the shared mass audience. This is audience:","options":{"A":"Fragmentation","B":"Gatekeeper","C":"A preferred reading","D":"Anchorage"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-06","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media rating is intended to measure:","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"the size or composition of an audience","C":"Primary institution","D":"Global conglomerate"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Ratings support scheduling, advertising and commissioning decisions.","reforge":{"stem":"A broadcaster compares how many households watched two programmes. Which measurement is being used?","options":{"A":"A media rating","B":"A stereotype","C":"A preferred reading","D":"A production code"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-07","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Curran and Seaton link media ownership to:","options":{"A":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","B":"Genre convention","C":"concentration of power and limits on diversity","D":"the speed of internet access"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Their political-economy approach examines ownership, control and plurality.","reforge":{"stem":"A study asks whether a small number of powerful owners can influence the range of viewpoints available in the media. This concerns:","options":{"A":"Media ownership and concentration","B":"guarantee a programme is profitable","C":"Lighting codes","D":"Narrative order"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-08","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Livingstone and Lunt argue that digital media regulation faces:","options":{"A":"no change from print regulation","B":"Cultivation — US audiences have been cultivated to prefer domestic content.","C":"only technical problems with cameras","D":"a gap between powerful global platforms and national regulation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Global online services complicate accountability and consumer protection.","reforge":{"stem":"A regulator must balance protecting audiences from harm with preserving freedom of expression. This is a challenge of media:","options":{"A":"Regulation","B":"a restriction on digital distribution","C":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","D":"Vertical editing"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-09","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Van Zoonen's feminist approach examines:","options":{"A":"how media construct gender and reproduce or challenge power","B":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","C":"Distribution channel","D":"Genre convention"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Gender representation can naturalise unequal roles and viewpoints.","reforge":{"stem":"An analysis asks how media texts construct gender and how those representations relate to social power. Which approach is relevant?","options":{"A":"Van Zoonen's feminist approach","B":"secrecy and menace in every context","C":"Cultivation only","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-10","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"The male gaze describes:","options":{"A":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","B":"representation that positions women as objects of a heterosexual male look","C":"Genre convention","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Mulvey links visual pleasure and narrative perspective to gendered power.","reforge":{"stem":"A film repeatedly positions the camera to encourage viewers to look at women through a heterosexual male viewpoint. This is the:","options":{"A":"Male gaze","B":"Distribution channel","C":"a completely unique character","D":"Cultivation only"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-11","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A cultural discount occurs when:","options":{"A":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","B":"Local regulation only","C":"a media product loses appeal across cultures because meanings are locally specific","D":"Audience isolation"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Language, humour and cultural references can reduce exportability.","reforge":{"stem":"A comedy loses some of its appeal when exported because its humour depends on local references. This is a cultural:","options":{"A":"Discount","B":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","C":"Rating","D":"Gatekeeping"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-12","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Globalisation can benefit media industries by:","options":{"A":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","B":"Gatekeeping","C":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","D":"opening access to larger markets and investment"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Global markets create opportunities but can also intensify cultural and economic inequalities.","reforge":{"stem":"A streaming platform distributes the same production across many national markets. This illustrates media:","options":{"A":"Globalisation","B":"Audience isolation","C":"Local regulation only","D":"Rating"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-13","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A gatekeeper is an actor who:","options":{"A":"selects or filters what reaches an audience","B":"Rating measures ideology","C":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content.","D":"Institutional ownership"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"Editors, platforms, algorithms and owners can all influence visibility.","reforge":{"stem":"An editor decides which stories appear on a newspaper's front page. The editor is acting as a:","options":{"A":"Gatekeeper","B":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","C":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","D":"User-generated producer"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-14","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"User-generated content is produced by:","options":{"A":"User-generated producer","B":"ordinary users rather than solely professional institutions","C":"Rating measures ideology","D":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"UGC can widen participation while raising issues of accuracy, moderation and exploitation.","reforge":{"stem":"A viewer uploads a review that becomes part of a platform's content. This is:","options":{"A":"User-generated content","B":"Institutional ownership","C":"A regulation code","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-15","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media algorithm may affect audiences by:","options":{"A":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content.","B":"A preferred reading","C":"ranking and recommending content based on data","D":"User-generated producer"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"Recommendation systems shape visibility and can reinforce existing preferences.","reforge":{"stem":"A platform ranks videos using viewing history and predicted interests. This shows how a media:","options":{"A":"Algorithm affects visibility","B":"Genre removes choice","C":"Rating measures ideology","D":"Stereotype controls production"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-16","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A regulation code is intended to:","options":{"A":"guarantee a programme is profitable","B":"replace all audience research","C":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","D":"set standards and responsibilities for media practice"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REGULATION-CONVERGENCE","scaffold":"Regulation may address harm, privacy, accuracy, fairness and access.","reforge":{"stem":"A broadcasting standard sets expectations for accuracy, privacy and protection from harm. This is a regulation:","options":{"A":"Code","B":"Neither — freedom of expression protects both from regulation.","C":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","D":"Vertical editing"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-E2-17","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media production's target audience is:","options":{"A":"the group the producers intend to reach","B":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","C":"Global conglomerate","D":"Primary institution"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Targeting influences content, platform, scheduling and marketing decisions.","reforge":{"stem":"A producer designs a programme specifically for teenage viewers interested in esports. This group is the programme's:","options":{"A":"Target audience","B":"Gatekeeper","C":"Regulator","D":"Ownership structure"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-002","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Curran and Seaton's power without responsibility model argues that:","options":{"A":"a small independent production","B":"The concentration of media ownership in the hands of a few large conglomerates limits the range of ideas and viewpoints in circulation, serving the interests of powerful owners rather than the public.","C":"Conglomeration — ownership across multiple different media sectors, creating cross-promotional synergy and market dominance. Curran & Seaton argue this limits diversity by concentrating power in fewer hands.","D":"Genre convention"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Curran & Seaton: media ownership concentrated in a few powerful companies (News Corp, Disney, Comcast). This concentration: reduces diversity of voices; creates commercial pressure prioritising profit over quality/public interest; enables owners to influence editorial line (Murdoch and The Sun's political positions). BUT: Curran also notes digital media has created some diversity (YouTube, podcasting). Hesmondhalgh critique: market logic dominates cultural industries regardless of ownership structure. Exam: evaluate — does concentration always limit diversity?","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media conglomerate that owns a news channel, a film studio and several newspapers is an example of:","options":{"A":"Narrative order","B":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","C":"Lighting codes","D":"Conglomeration — ownership across multiple different media sectors, creating cross-promotional synergy and market dominance. Curran & Seaton argue this limits diversity by concentrating power in fewer hands."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-004","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"In media studies, 'representation' means:","options":{"A":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","B":"a completely unique character","C":"The process by which media construct versions of reality — selecting, combining and presenting elements to produce meanings about social groups, events and ideas. Representations are constructions, not reflections, of reality.","D":"Distribution channel"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Representation: media don't reflect reality — they RE-present it through selection and construction. Key ideas: (1) Re-presentation — same reality can be constructed differently (tabloid vs broadsheet coverage of same event). (2) Stereotyping — shortcut representations that can reinforce or challenge ideological assumptions. (3) Power — who controls representation? Whose perspective is centred? (4) Stuart Hall's 'the spectacle of the other' — difference is marked, exoticised, othered. Exam always asks for application to specific texts — don't just define, analyse HOW the text constructs representation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Two newspapers cover the same protest: one uses the headline 'Passionate campaigners demand action' and another uses 'Troublemakers disrupt city'. This illustrates which concept?","options":{"A":"Re-presentation — the same event is constructed differently through language choices, encoding different preferred readings about protesters and their legitimacy. Neither is a neutral 'reflection' of the event.","B":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","C":"Cultivation only","D":"Genre convention"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-006","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Van Zoonen's feminist media theory argues that:","options":{"A":"Distribution channel","B":"The hypodermic model","C":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","D":"Gender is a cultural construction performed and perpetuated through representation — media texts produce and reproduce gendered identities by consistently presenting femininity and masculinity in particular ways, often through the 'male gaze'."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Van Zoonen (1994) feminist media theory: gender is not natural but constructed through cultural practices including media representation. The 'male gaze' (Mulvey — applied to film originally): camera positions women as spectacle for male viewer's pleasure. Women coded as objects rather than subjects. BUT: Van Zoonen acknowledges female audiences actively resist and renegotiate representations. Contemporary relevance: online content creation, OnlyFans debate — exploitation or empowerment? Intersectionality (Crenshaw) — gender intersects with race, class in representations. Exam: avoid stating all representation is negative — evaluate complexity.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A music video features a female artist singing from a stationary position while the camera repeatedly focuses on her body in fragmented close-ups. Van Zoonen and Mulvey would analyse this as:","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"An example of the male gaze — the camera's fragmentation of the female body positions her as visual spectacle for a presumed male viewer's pleasure rather than as a subject with agency, reproducing a patriarchal representation of femininity.","C":"Cultivation only","D":"Genre convention"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-008","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Blumler and Katz's uses and gratifications theory argues that:","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","C":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","D":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Blumler & Katz (1974) uses and gratifications: shifts focus from 'what does media DO to audiences?' to 'what do audiences DO with media?' Four needs: (1) Diversion/escape — entertainment, relaxation; (2) Personal relationships — companionship, social interaction via media (parasocial relationships); (3) Personal identity — finding values and role models in texts; (4) Surveillance/information — understanding the world. Modern examples: binge-watching (escape), following influencers (parasocial), using TikTok for news. Criticism: assumes audiences are always rational and purposeful; ignores ideological effects; ignores how industry shapes 'choice'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student watches a reality TV show featuring young people from a similar background and identifies with a contestant who overcomes adversity. Uses and gratifications would explain this as:","options":{"A":"The hypodermic model","B":"Production budget","C":"The personal identity function — the student is actively using the text to explore and reinforce their own values and sense of self by identifying with a character whose experiences resonate with their own.","D":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-010","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Anchorage is used to:","options":{"A":"a legal ownership document","B":"limit or direct the meaning of an image","C":"how it positions and speaks to its audience","D":"Institutional ownership"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Captions and headlines can guide how an image is read.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A caption directs viewers to interpret an ambiguous photograph as evidence of hope. The caption provides:","options":{"A":"A mode of address","B":"Anchorage","C":"Ownership pattern","D":"A rating"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-012","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"a media stereotype is:","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"a completely unique character","C":"a simplified and repeated representation of a group","D":"Distribution channel"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Stereotypes can make groups seem fixed, natural or easily recognisable.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A sitcom repeatedly presents one social group using a simplified and predictable image. This is a:","options":{"A":"Stereotype","B":"Genre convention","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"Cultivation only"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-014","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"The preferred reading of a media text is:","options":{"A":"removes the need for marketing","B":"the geography of a whole setting","C":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","D":"the interpretation most encouraged by its creators"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Audiences may accept, negotiate or oppose a preferred reading.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A producer encodes a documentary to encourage viewers to support its political argument. The intended reading is:","options":{"A":"Preferred","B":"Oppositional","C":"Accidental","D":"Denotative"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-016","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A high-key lighting style commonly suggests:","options":{"A":"brightness, openness or a positive mood","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"the geography of a whole setting","D":"removes the need for marketing"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Lighting meaning depends on context but high-key images often appear bright and accessible.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A daytime advert uses bright, shadow-free lighting to create an upbeat mood. This is:","options":{"A":"High-key lighting","B":"Naturalistic underexposure","C":"Documentary-only","D":"Underlighting"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-018","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A non-linear narrative presents events:","options":{"A":"the geography of a whole setting","B":"out of chronological order","C":"Negotiated rejection","D":"Low-key chiaroscuro"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Flashbacks and parallel timelines can make audiences reconstruct narrative order.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A drama opens with the ending before returning to earlier events. Its narrative structure is:","options":{"A":"Non-linear","B":"secrecy and menace in every context","C":"Documentary-only","D":"Live broadcast"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-020","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Vertical integration occurs when a company controls:","options":{"A":"Public service broadcasting","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"different stages of production and distribution","D":"broadcast only to subscribers"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Control across stages can reduce costs and dependence on other firms.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A company owns the studio, distributor and platform used to release its films. This is:","options":{"A":"Vertical integration","B":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","C":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","D":"User-generated content"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-022","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media conglomerate is:","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"Genre convention","C":"Single-text audience","D":"a large company with interests across media markets"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Conglomerates may own multiple brands and platforms.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A parent corporation owns radio, newspapers, television and film businesses. It is a:","options":{"A":"Media conglomerate","B":"Narrative order","C":"Lighting codes","D":"the biological effects of colour"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-024","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media text's mode of address refers to:","options":{"A":"how it positions and speaks to its audience","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"Institutional ownership","D":"A mode of address"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Language, tone and design create a relationship with an implied audience.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A presenter speaks directly to the camera in relaxed, informal language. This establishes the text's:","options":{"A":"Mode of address","B":"Media rating","C":"Ownership pattern","D":"A rating"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-026","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"In media industry terms, 'vertical integration' means: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","B":"A media company owning businesses at multiple stages of the same production and distribution chain — for example, a studio that produces films, owns the distribution network and operates the cinemas that screen them.","C":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Vertical integration: ownership across different stages of one supply chain (production → distribution → exhibition). Example: Disney produces content (studio), distributes it (Disney+, theatrical), and licenses merchandise — controlling the whole chain. Benefits: cost efficiencies, control over release windows and pricing, guaranteed outlets. Risks: regulatory scrutiny (anti-competition). Contrast with horizontal integration: buying competitors at the SAME level (Disney acquiring 21st Century Fox — both studios). Exam: always specify which type and why it matters for diversity/power.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A record label that records artists, distributes their music through its own streaming platform, and books them into venues it owns is an example of:","options":{"A":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","B":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","C":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","D":"Vertical integration — the label controls production (recording), distribution (streaming) and exhibition (venues), owning the entire supply chain from creation to audience delivery."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-028","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"How should media convergence be understood?","options":{"A":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"The merging of previously distinct media technologies, industries and content forms — so that a single device (smartphone) can deliver television, radio, newspapers, social media and interactive games, collapsing former boundaries between media types.","D":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Convergence (Jenkins): technological — devices deliver multiple media types (phone = TV + radio + newspaper + camera). Industrial — companies merge across formerly separate sectors (telecoms + media + tech: Apple, Google, Amazon all now media companies). Content — formats blend (podcast + live event + YouTube series). Cultural — audiences move fluidly between platforms for the same story (transmedia storytelling). Consequences: disrupts traditional regulatory boundaries; enables new forms of audience participation; challenges business models of legacy media (print, linear TV). Livingstone & Lunt: regulation designed for distinct sectors can't keep up with converged landscape.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A viewer watches a live football match on their phone, simultaneously discussing it on social media and accessing statistics via a club app. This scenario illustrates:","options":{"A":"Media convergence — a single device delivers formerly separate media experiences (television, social media, data journalism) simultaneously, and the viewer participates rather than passively consuming.","B":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","C":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","D":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-030","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"In Hesmondhalgh's analysis of cultural industries, the 'cultural discount' refers to: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"Local regulation only","B":"Audience isolation","C":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","D":"The loss of value a cultural product suffers when exported to a different cultural market"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Cultural discount (Hoskins & Mirus, developed by Hesmondhalgh): when media crosses cultural boundaries, local specificity reduces its value to foreign audiences. US media minimises cultural discount by: producing for large domestic market (economies of scale), using universal genres (action, romance), English language advantage, minimal cultural specificity. This gives US content a competitive advantage globally — other nations' content faces higher discount barriers. Counter-examples: Korean Wave (Hallyu) — K-drama, K-pop have reduced their cultural discount through universal genre conventions + distinctive aesthetics. Exam: connect to global media dominance debates.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A British sitcom with jokes about specific UK institutions (NHS, the BBC, British politics) struggles to attract audiences in the United States. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"Gatekeeping","B":"The cultural discount — UK-specific references reduce the sitcom's appeal abroad","C":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","D":"Rating"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-032","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"In the context of digital media, 'user-generated content' (UGC) challenges traditional media industry models because: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"It blurs the distinction between producer and audience — anyone with a smartphone can create and distribute content globally, undermining the gatekeeping power of traditional media institutions and creating a competitive content environment that legacy media cannot easily replicate.","B":"Stereotype controls production","C":"Rating measures ideology","D":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"UGC and the 'prosumer' (Toffler): audiences as producers. Platforms enabling UGC: YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Twitch, podcasting. Challenges to legacy media: (1) Gatekeeping disrupted — publishers no longer control what reaches audiences; (2) Revenue diverted — advertising follows attention, which increasingly goes to UGC platforms; (3) Speed — UGC often breaks news faster than journalists; (4) Scale — YouTube alone uploads 500 hours of video per minute. BUT: quality control, misinformation, algorithmic curation create new problems (Livingstone & Lunt). Gauntlett's 'making is connecting' — cultural participation is democratised. Critical view: platform companies (not creators) capture most of the value; precarious 'creator economy'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A teenager's home video of a local flood goes viral on TikTok, reaching 10 million viewers before any news broadcaster has covered the story. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"Institutional ownership","B":"User-generated producer","C":"How UGC bypasses gatekeeping — the teenager is both producer and distributor","D":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-034","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Uses and gratifications theory treats audiences as: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"active users selecting media to meet needs","C":"Reception theory","D":"Two-step flow"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Audiences may seek information, identity, social interaction or entertainment.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Uses and gratifications theory treats audiences as:","options":{"A":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","B":"Regulatory code","C":"Production budget","D":"active users selecting media to meet needs"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-036","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"the hypodermic needle model assumes media messages are: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","B":"Reception theory","C":"directly injected into passive audiences","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"It is a simple effects model and is criticised for underestimating audience activity.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"the hypodermic needle model assumes media messages are:","options":{"A":"directly injected into passive audiences","B":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","C":"Regulatory code","D":"Two-step flow"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-038","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Audience fragmentation means: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"A preferred reading","C":"Ownership structure","D":"audiences are divided across more channels and platforms"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Digital choice can make audiences more dispersed and specialised.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Audience fragmentation means:","options":{"A":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","B":"audiences are divided across more channels and platforms","C":"Primary institution","D":"A production code"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-040","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Curran and Seaton link media ownership to: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"concentration of power and limits on diversity","B":"Genre convention","C":"Lighting codes","D":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Their political-economy approach examines ownership, control and plurality.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Curran and Seaton link media ownership to:","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"Narrative order","C":"concentration of power and limits on diversity","D":"the speed of internet access"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-042","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Van Zoonen's feminist approach examines: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","B":"how media construct gender and reproduce or challenge power","C":"Distribution channel","D":"Genre convention"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Gender representation can naturalise unequal roles and viewpoints.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Van Zoonen's feminist approach examines:","options":{"A":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","B":"The hypodermic model","C":"Cultivation only","D":"how media construct gender and reproduce or challenge power"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-044","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A cultural discount occurs when: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","B":"Audience isolation","C":"a media product loses appeal across cultures because meanings are locally specific","D":"Cultivation — US audiences have been cultivated to prefer domestic content."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Language, humour and cultural references can reduce exportability.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A cultural discount occurs when:","options":{"A":"a media product loses appeal across cultures because meanings are locally specific","B":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","C":"Gatekeeping","D":"Rating"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-046","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A gatekeeper is an actor who: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"User-generated producer","B":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content.","C":"Genre removes choice","D":"selects or filters what reaches an audience"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"Editors, platforms, algorithms and owners can all influence visibility.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A gatekeeper is an actor who:","options":{"A":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","B":"selects or filters what reaches an audience","C":"Institutional ownership","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-048","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media algorithm may affect audiences by: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"ranking and recommending content based on data","B":"Rating measures ideology","C":"Institutional ownership","D":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"Recommendation systems shape visibility and can reinforce existing preferences.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media algorithm may affect audiences by:","options":{"A":"Genre removes choice","B":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","C":"ranking and recommending content based on data","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-050","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media production's target audience is: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"the group the producers intend to reach","C":"A preferred reading","D":"A production code"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Targeting influences content, platform, scheduling and marketing decisions.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media production's target audience is:","options":{"A":"Primary institution","B":"Ownership structure","C":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","D":"the group the producers intend to reach"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-052","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Curran and Seaton's power without responsibility model argues that: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","B":"Lighting codes","C":"The concentration of media ownership in the hands of a few large conglomerates limits the range of ideas and viewpoints in circulation, serving the interests of powerful owners rather than the public.","D":"Narrative order"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Curran & Seaton: media ownership concentrated in a few powerful companies (News Corp, Disney, Comcast). This concentration: reduces diversity of voices; creates commercial pressure prioritising profit over quality/public interest; enables owners to influence editorial line (Murdoch and The Sun's political positions). BUT: Curran also notes digital media has created some diversity (YouTube, podcasting). Hesmondhalgh critique: market logic dominates cultural industries regardless of ownership structure. Exam: evaluate — does concentration always limit diversity?","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media conglomerate that owns a news channel, a film studio and several newspapers is an example of:","options":{"A":"Conglomeration — ownership across multiple different media sectors, creating cross-promotional synergy and market dominance. Curran & Seaton argue this limits diversity by concentrating power in fewer hands.","B":"Genre convention","C":"Single-text audience","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-054","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"In media studies, 'representation' means: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","B":"Genre convention","C":"a completely unique character","D":"The process by which media construct versions of reality — selecting, combining and presenting elements to produce meanings about social groups, events and ideas. Representations are constructions, not reflections, of reality."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Representation: media don't reflect reality — they RE-present it through selection and construction. Key ideas: (1) Re-presentation — same reality can be constructed differently (tabloid vs broadsheet coverage of same event). (2) Stereotyping — shortcut representations that can reinforce or challenge ideological assumptions. (3) Power — who controls representation? Whose perspective is centred? (4) Stuart Hall's 'the spectacle of the other' — difference is marked, exoticised, othered. Exam always asks for application to specific texts — don't just define, analyse HOW the text constructs representation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Two newspapers cover the same protest: one uses the headline 'Passionate campaigners demand action' and another uses 'Troublemakers disrupt city'. This illustrates which concept?","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"Re-presentation — the same event is constructed differently through language choices, encoding different preferred readings about protesters and their legitimacy. Neither is a neutral 'reflection' of the event.","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"Cultivation only"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-056","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Van Zoonen's feminist media theory argues that: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"Gender is a cultural construction performed and perpetuated through representation — media texts produce and reproduce gendered identities by consistently presenting femininity and masculinity in particular ways, often through the 'male gaze'.","B":"Genre convention","C":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","D":"Distribution channel"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Van Zoonen (1994) feminist media theory: gender is not natural but constructed through cultural practices including media representation. The 'male gaze' (Mulvey — applied to film originally): camera positions women as spectacle for male viewer's pleasure. Women coded as objects rather than subjects. BUT: Van Zoonen acknowledges female audiences actively resist and renegotiate representations. Contemporary relevance: online content creation, OnlyFans debate — exploitation or empowerment? Intersectionality (Crenshaw) — gender intersects with race, class in representations. Exam: avoid stating all representation is negative — evaluate complexity.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A music video features a female artist singing from a stationary position while the camera repeatedly focuses on her body in fragmented close-ups. Van Zoonen and Mulvey would analyse this as:","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"The hypodermic model","C":"An example of the male gaze — the camera's fragmentation of the female body positions her as visual spectacle for a presumed male viewer's pleasure rather than as a subject with agency, reproducing a patriarchal representation of femininity.","D":"Cultivation only"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-058","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Blumler and Katz's uses and gratifications theory argues that: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","B":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","C":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","D":"Production budget"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Blumler & Katz (1974) uses and gratifications: shifts focus from 'what does media DO to audiences?' to 'what do audiences DO with media?' Four needs: (1) Diversion/escape — entertainment, relaxation; (2) Personal relationships — companionship, social interaction via media (parasocial relationships); (3) Personal identity — finding values and role models in texts; (4) Surveillance/information — understanding the world. Modern examples: binge-watching (escape), following influencers (parasocial), using TikTok for news. Criticism: assumes audiences are always rational and purposeful; ignores ideological effects; ignores how industry shapes 'choice'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student watches a reality TV show featuring young people from a similar background and identifies with a contestant who overcomes adversity. Uses and gratifications would explain this as:","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"The personal identity function — the student is actively using the text to explore and reinforce their own values and sense of self by identifying with a character whose experiences resonate with their own."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-060","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"anchorage is used to: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","B":"A mode of address","C":"limit or direct the meaning of an image","D":"A rating"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Captions and headlines can guide how an image is read.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"anchorage is used to:","options":{"A":"limit or direct the meaning of an image","B":"a legal ownership document","C":"Ownership pattern","D":"how it positions and speaks to its audience"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-062","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media stereotype is: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"Genre convention","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"a simplified and repeated representation of a group"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Stereotypes can make groups seem fixed, natural or easily recognisable.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media stereotype is:","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"a simplified and repeated representation of a group","C":"Distribution channel","D":"Cultivation only"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-064","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"The preferred reading of a media text is: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"the interpretation most encouraged by its creators","B":"A public service announcement","C":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","D":"Oppositional reading"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Audiences may accept, negotiate or oppose a preferred reading.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"The preferred reading of a media text is:","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"Naturalistic underexposure","C":"the interpretation most encouraged by its creators","D":"removes the need for marketing"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-066","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A high-key lighting style commonly suggests: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","B":"brightness, openness or a positive mood","C":"removes the need for marketing","D":"A public service announcement"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Lighting meaning depends on context but high-key images often appear bright and accessible.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A high-key lighting style commonly suggests:","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"Naturalistic underexposure","C":"the geography of a whole setting","D":"brightness, openness or a positive mood"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-068","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A non-linear narrative presents events: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Low-key chiaroscuro","B":"the geography of a whole setting","C":"out of chronological order","D":"Low-key lighting"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Flashbacks and parallel timelines can make audiences reconstruct narrative order.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A non-linear narrative presents events:","options":{"A":"out of chronological order","B":"A genre institution","C":"Documentary-only","D":"secrecy and menace in every context"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-070","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Vertical integration occurs when a company controls: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"only two unrelated products","B":"Public service broadcasting","C":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","D":"different stages of production and distribution"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Control across stages can reduce costs and dependence on other firms.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Vertical integration occurs when a company controls:","options":{"A":"only two unrelated products","B":"different stages of production and distribution","C":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","D":"User-generated content"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-072","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media conglomerate is: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"a large company with interests across media markets","B":"Single-text audience","C":"Narrative order","D":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Conglomerates may own multiple brands and platforms.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media conglomerate is:","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"Lighting codes","C":"a large company with interests across media markets","D":"Genre convention"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-074","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media text's mode of address refers to: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"A rating","B":"how it positions and speaks to its audience","C":"Ownership pattern","D":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Language, tone and design create a relationship with an implied audience.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media text's mode of address refers to:","options":{"A":"A company expanding into the same level of production as its competitors.","B":"Institutional ownership","C":"A mode of address","D":"how it positions and speaks to its audience"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-076","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"In media industry terms, 'vertical integration' means: (alternative exam formulation) (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","B":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","C":"A media company owning businesses at multiple stages of the same production and distribution chain — for example, a studio that produces films, owns the distribution network and operates the cinemas that screen them.","D":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Vertical integration: ownership across different stages of one supply chain (production → distribution → exhibition). Example: Disney produces content (studio), distributes it (Disney+, theatrical), and licenses merchandise — controlling the whole chain. Benefits: cost efficiencies, control over release windows and pricing, guaranteed outlets. Risks: regulatory scrutiny (anti-competition). Contrast with horizontal integration: buying competitors at the SAME level (Disney acquiring 21st Century Fox — both studios). Exam: always specify which type and why it matters for diversity/power.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A record label that records artists, distributes their music through its own streaming platform, and books them into venues it owns is an example of:","options":{"A":"Vertical integration — the label controls production (recording), distribution (streaming) and exhibition (venues), owning the entire supply chain from creation to audience delivery.","B":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","C":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","D":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-078","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"How should media convergence be understood? (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","D":"The merging of previously distinct media technologies, industries and content forms — so that a single device (smartphone) can deliver television, radio, newspapers, social media and interactive games, collapsing former boundaries between media types."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Convergence (Jenkins): technological — devices deliver multiple media types (phone = TV + radio + newspaper + camera). Industrial — companies merge across formerly separate sectors (telecoms + media + tech: Apple, Google, Amazon all now media companies). Content — formats blend (podcast + live event + YouTube series). Cultural — audiences move fluidly between platforms for the same story (transmedia storytelling). Consequences: disrupts traditional regulatory boundaries; enables new forms of audience participation; challenges business models of legacy media (print, linear TV). Livingstone & Lunt: regulation designed for distinct sectors can't keep up with converged landscape.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A viewer watches a live football match on their phone, simultaneously discussing it on social media and accessing statistics via a club app. This scenario illustrates:","options":{"A":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","B":"Media convergence — a single device delivers formerly separate media experiences (television, social media, data journalism) simultaneously, and the viewer participates rather than passively consuming.","C":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","D":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-080","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"In Hesmondhalgh's analysis of cultural industries, the 'cultural discount' refers to: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The loss of value a cultural product suffers when exported to a different cultural market","B":"Audience isolation","C":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","D":"Gatekeeping"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Cultural discount (Hoskins & Mirus, developed by Hesmondhalgh): when media crosses cultural boundaries, local specificity reduces its value to foreign audiences. US media minimises cultural discount by: producing for large domestic market (economies of scale), using universal genres (action, romance), English language advantage, minimal cultural specificity. This gives US content a competitive advantage globally — other nations' content faces higher discount barriers. Counter-examples: Korean Wave (Hallyu) — K-drama, K-pop have reduced their cultural discount through universal genre conventions + distinctive aesthetics. Exam: connect to global media dominance debates.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A British sitcom with jokes about specific UK institutions (NHS, the BBC, British politics) struggles to attract audiences in the United States. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"Rating","B":"Local regulation only","C":"The cultural discount — UK-specific references reduce the sitcom's appeal abroad","D":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-082","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"In the context of digital media, 'user-generated content' (UGC) challenges traditional media industry models because: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","B":"It blurs the distinction between producer and audience — anyone with a smartphone can create and distribute content globally, undermining the gatekeeping power of traditional media institutions and creating a competitive content environment that legacy media cannot easily replicate.","C":"Stereotype controls production","D":"Rating measures ideology"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"UGC and the 'prosumer' (Toffler): audiences as producers. Platforms enabling UGC: YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Twitch, podcasting. Challenges to legacy media: (1) Gatekeeping disrupted — publishers no longer control what reaches audiences; (2) Revenue diverted — advertising follows attention, which increasingly goes to UGC platforms; (3) Speed — UGC often breaks news faster than journalists; (4) Scale — YouTube alone uploads 500 hours of video per minute. BUT: quality control, misinformation, algorithmic curation create new problems (Livingstone & Lunt). Gauntlett's 'making is connecting' — cultural participation is democratised. Critical view: platform companies (not creators) capture most of the value; precarious 'creator economy'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A teenager's home video of a local flood goes viral on TikTok, reaching 10 million viewers before any news broadcaster has covered the story. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"Institutional ownership","B":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","C":"User-generated producer","D":"How UGC bypasses gatekeeping — the teenager is both producer and distributor"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-084","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"uses and gratifications theory treats audiences as: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"Two-step flow","C":"active users selecting media to meet needs","D":"Reception theory"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Audiences may seek information, identity, social interaction or entertainment.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"uses and gratifications theory treats audiences as:","options":{"A":"active users selecting media to meet needs","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"Production budget","D":"Regulatory code"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-086","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"The hypodermic needle model assumes media messages are: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"Two-step flow","B":"Regulatory code","C":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","D":"directly injected into passive audiences"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"It is a simple effects model and is criticised for underestimating audience activity.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"The hypodermic needle model assumes media messages are:","options":{"A":"Production budget","B":"directly injected into passive audiences","C":"Reception theory","D":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-088","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Audience fragmentation means: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"audiences are divided across more channels and platforms","B":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","C":"A production code","D":"Global conglomerate"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Digital choice can make audiences more dispersed and specialised.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Audience fragmentation means:","options":{"A":"Ownership structure","B":"Primary institution","C":"audiences are divided across more channels and platforms","D":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-090","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Curran and Seaton link media ownership to: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"Single-text audience","B":"concentration of power and limits on diversity","C":"Narrative order","D":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Their political-economy approach examines ownership, control and plurality.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Curran and Seaton link media ownership to:","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"Genre convention","C":"Lighting codes","D":"concentration of power and limits on diversity"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-092","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Van Zoonen's feminist approach examines: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","B":"Cultivation only","C":"how media construct gender and reproduce or challenge power","D":"Distribution channel"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Gender representation can naturalise unequal roles and viewpoints.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Van Zoonen's feminist approach examines:","options":{"A":"how media construct gender and reproduce or challenge power","B":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"Genre convention"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-094","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A cultural discount occurs when: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"Rating","B":"Gatekeeping","C":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","D":"a media product loses appeal across cultures because meanings are locally specific"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Language, humour and cultural references can reduce exportability.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A cultural discount occurs when:","options":{"A":"Local regulation only","B":"a media product loses appeal across cultures because meanings are locally specific","C":"Audience isolation","D":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-096","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A gatekeeper is an actor who: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"selects or filters what reaches an audience","B":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content.","C":"A preferred reading","D":"Institutional ownership"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"Editors, platforms, algorithms and owners can all influence visibility.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A gatekeeper is an actor who:","options":{"A":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","B":"Genre removes choice","C":"selects or filters what reaches an audience","D":"User-generated producer"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-098","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media algorithm may affect audiences by: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content.","B":"ranking and recommending content based on data","C":"Institutional ownership","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"Recommendation systems shape visibility and can reinforce existing preferences.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media algorithm may affect audiences by:","options":{"A":"Genre removes choice","B":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","C":"User-generated producer","D":"ranking and recommending content based on data"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-100","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media production's target audience is: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"Primary institution","C":"the group the producers intend to reach","D":"Ownership structure"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Targeting influences content, platform, scheduling and marketing decisions.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media production's target audience is:","options":{"A":"the group the producers intend to reach","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"Global conglomerate","D":"A production code"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-102","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Curran and Seaton's power without responsibility model argues that: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"a small independent production","B":"Genre convention","C":"Conglomeration — ownership across multiple different media sectors, creating cross-promotional synergy and market dominance. Curran & Seaton argue this limits diversity by concentrating power in fewer hands.","D":"The concentration of media ownership in the hands of a few large conglomerates limits the range of ideas and viewpoints in circulation, serving the interests of powerful owners rather than the public."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Curran & Seaton: media ownership concentrated in a few powerful companies (News Corp, Disney, Comcast). This concentration: reduces diversity of voices; creates commercial pressure prioritising profit over quality/public interest; enables owners to influence editorial line (Murdoch and The Sun's political positions). BUT: Curran also notes digital media has created some diversity (YouTube, podcasting). Hesmondhalgh critique: market logic dominates cultural industries regardless of ownership structure. Exam: evaluate — does concentration always limit diversity?","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Which option is defensible when the context changes? A media conglomerate that owns a news channel, a film studio and several newspapers is an example of:","options":{"A":"Narrative order","B":"Conglomeration — ownership across multiple different media sectors, creating cross-promotional synergy and market dominance. Curran & Seaton argue this limits diversity by concentrating power in fewer hands.","C":"Lighting codes","D":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-104","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"In media studies, 'representation' means: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"The process by which media construct versions of reality — selecting, combining and presenting elements to produce meanings about social groups, events and ideas. Representations are constructions, not reflections, of reality.","B":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","C":"Distribution channel","D":"are created only by audiences"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Representation: media don't reflect reality — they RE-present it through selection and construction. Key ideas: (1) Re-presentation — same reality can be constructed differently (tabloid vs broadsheet coverage of same event). (2) Stereotyping — shortcut representations that can reinforce or challenge ideological assumptions. (3) Power — who controls representation? Whose perspective is centred? (4) Stuart Hall's 'the spectacle of the other' — difference is marked, exoticised, othered. Exam always asks for application to specific texts — don't just define, analyse HOW the text constructs representation.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Two newspapers cover the same protest: one uses the headline 'Passionate campaigners demand action' and another uses 'Troublemakers disrupt city'. This illustrates which concept?","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"Cultivation only","C":"Re-presentation — the same event is constructed differently through language choices, encoding different preferred readings about protesters and their legitimacy. Neither is a neutral 'reflection' of the event.","D":"Genre convention"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-106","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"van Zoonen's feminist media theory argues that: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"Distribution channel","B":"Gender is a cultural construction performed and perpetuated through representation — media texts produce and reproduce gendered identities by consistently presenting femininity and masculinity in particular ways, often through the 'male gaze'.","C":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Van Zoonen (1994) feminist media theory: gender is not natural but constructed through cultural practices including media representation. The 'male gaze' (Mulvey — applied to film originally): camera positions women as spectacle for male viewer's pleasure. Women coded as objects rather than subjects. BUT: Van Zoonen acknowledges female audiences actively resist and renegotiate representations. Contemporary relevance: online content creation, OnlyFans debate — exploitation or empowerment? Intersectionality (Crenshaw) — gender intersects with race, class in representations. Exam: avoid stating all representation is negative — evaluate complexity.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A music video features a female artist singing from a stationary position while the camera repeatedly focuses on her body in fragmented close-ups. Van Zoonen and Mulvey would analyse this as:","options":{"A":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","B":"Genre convention","C":"Cultivation only","D":"An example of the male gaze — the camera's fragmentation of the female body positions her as visual spectacle for a presumed male viewer's pleasure rather than as a subject with agency, reproducing a patriarchal representation of femininity."},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-108","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Blumler and Katz's uses and gratifications theory argues that: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"Audiences process media effects in two cognitive stages: conscious and unconscious.","B":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","C":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience.","D":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Blumler & Katz (1974) uses and gratifications: shifts focus from 'what does media DO to audiences?' to 'what do audiences DO with media?' Four needs: (1) Diversion/escape — entertainment, relaxation; (2) Personal relationships — companionship, social interaction via media (parasocial relationships); (3) Personal identity — finding values and role models in texts; (4) Surveillance/information — understanding the world. Modern examples: binge-watching (escape), following influencers (parasocial), using TikTok for news. Criticism: assumes audiences are always rational and purposeful; ignores ideological effects; ignores how industry shapes 'choice'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A student watches a reality TV show featuring young people from a similar background and identifies with a contestant who overcomes adversity. Uses and gratifications would explain this as:","options":{"A":"The personal identity function — the student is actively using the text to explore and reinforce their own values and sense of self by identifying with a character whose experiences resonate with their own.","B":"The hypodermic model","C":"Production budget","D":"Audiences are active users of media who choose texts purposefully to satisfy specific needs: information/surveillance, personal identity, social integration and entertainment. The audience uses the media, rather than the media using the audience."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-110","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Anchorage is used to: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"a legal ownership document","B":"Institutional ownership","C":"how it positions and speaks to its audience","D":"limit or direct the meaning of an image"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Captions and headlines can guide how an image is read.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A caption directs viewers to interpret an ambiguous photograph as evidence of hope. The caption provides:","options":{"A":"A mode of address","B":"Anchorage","C":"Ownership pattern","D":"A rating"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-112","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media stereotype is: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"a simplified and repeated representation of a group","B":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","C":"Distribution channel","D":"a completely unique character"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Stereotypes can make groups seem fixed, natural or easily recognisable.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A sitcom repeatedly presents one social group using a simplified and predictable image. This is a:","options":{"A":"Stereotype","B":"Genre convention","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"Cultivation only"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-114","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"The preferred reading of a media text is: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"removes the need for marketing","B":"the interpretation most encouraged by its creators","C":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","D":"the geography of a whole setting"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Audiences may accept, negotiate or oppose a preferred reading.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A producer encodes a documentary to encourage viewers to support its political argument. The intended reading is:","options":{"A":"Preferred","B":"Oppositional","C":"Accidental","D":"Denotative"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-116","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A high-key lighting style commonly suggests: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"removes the need for marketing","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"brightness, openness or a positive mood","D":"the geography of a whole setting"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Lighting meaning depends on context but high-key images often appear bright and accessible.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A daytime advert uses bright, shadow-free lighting to create an upbeat mood. This is:","options":{"A":"High-key lighting","B":"Naturalistic underexposure","C":"Documentary-only","D":"Underlighting"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-118","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A non-linear narrative presents events: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"the geography of a whole setting","B":"Low-key chiaroscuro","C":"Negotiated rejection","D":"out of chronological order"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-LANGUAGE-FORM","scaffold":"Flashbacks and parallel timelines can make audiences reconstruct narrative order.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A drama opens with the ending before returning to earlier events. Its narrative structure is:","options":{"A":"Non-linear","B":"secrecy and menace in every context","C":"Documentary-only","D":"Live broadcast"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-120","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Vertical integration occurs when a company controls: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"different stages of production and distribution","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"broadcast only to subscribers","D":"Public service broadcasting"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Control across stages can reduce costs and dependence on other firms.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A company owns the studio, distributor and platform used to release its films. This is:","options":{"A":"Vertical integration","B":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","C":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","D":"User-generated content"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-122","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media conglomerate is: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","B":"a large company with interests across media markets","C":"Single-text audience","D":"Genre convention"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Conglomerates may own multiple brands and platforms.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A parent corporation owns radio, newspapers, television and film businesses. It is a:","options":{"A":"Media conglomerate","B":"Narrative order","C":"Lighting codes","D":"the biological effects of colour"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-124","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media text's mode of address refers to: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"A mode of address","B":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","C":"how it positions and speaks to its audience","D":"Institutional ownership"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-SEMIOTICS","scaffold":"Language, tone and design create a relationship with an implied audience.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A presenter speaks directly to the camera in relaxed, informal language. This establishes the text's:","options":{"A":"Mode of address","B":"Media rating","C":"Ownership pattern","D":"A rating"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-126","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"In media industry terms, 'vertical integration' means: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","B":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","C":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company.","D":"A media company owning businesses at multiple stages of the same production and distribution chain — for example, a studio that produces films, owns the distribution network and operates the cinemas that screen them."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Vertical integration: ownership across different stages of one supply chain (production → distribution → exhibition). Example: Disney produces content (studio), distributes it (Disney+, theatrical), and licenses merchandise — controlling the whole chain. Benefits: cost efficiencies, control over release windows and pricing, guaranteed outlets. Risks: regulatory scrutiny (anti-competition). Contrast with horizontal integration: buying competitors at the SAME level (Disney acquiring 21st Century Fox — both studios). Exam: always specify which type and why it matters for diversity/power.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A record label that records artists, distributes their music through its own streaming platform, and books them into venues it owns is an example of:","options":{"A":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","B":"Vertical integration — the label controls production (recording), distribution (streaming) and exhibition (venues), owning the entire supply chain from creation to audience delivery.","C":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","D":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-128","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"How should media convergence be understood? (second formulation)","options":{"A":"The merging of previously distinct media technologies, industries and content forms — so that a single device (smartphone) can deliver television, radio, newspapers, social media and interactive games, collapsing former boundaries between media types.","B":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","C":"Synergy means that a broadcaster receives public funding and must provide balanced coverage across its services; it does not describe a conglomerate using several holdings to cross-promote one media product.","D":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-INDUSTRY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Convergence (Jenkins): technological — devices deliver multiple media types (phone = TV + radio + newspaper + camera). Industrial — companies merge across formerly separate sectors (telecoms + media + tech: Apple, Google, Amazon all now media companies). Content — formats blend (podcast + live event + YouTube series). Cultural — audiences move fluidly between platforms for the same story (transmedia storytelling). Consequences: disrupts traditional regulatory boundaries; enables new forms of audience participation; challenges business models of legacy media (print, linear TV). Livingstone & Lunt: regulation designed for distinct sectors can't keep up with converged landscape.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A viewer watches a live football match on their phone, simultaneously discussing it on social media and accessing statistics via a club app. This scenario illustrates:","options":{"A":"The regulatory requirement for balanced coverage across different media outlets.","B":"Channel 4 fulfilling a commercial ratings strategy would mean cancelling programmes that attract small audiences, even when those programmes provide cultural value and represent communities that commercial broadcasters may overlook.","C":"Media convergence — a single device delivers formerly separate media experiences (television, social media, data journalism) simultaneously, and the viewer participates rather than passively consuming.","D":"Vertical integration — multiple platforms owned by one company."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-130","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"in Hesmondhalgh's analysis of cultural industries, the 'cultural discount' refers to: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"Local regulation only","B":"The loss of value a cultural product suffers when exported to a different cultural market","C":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","D":"Audience isolation"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Cultural discount (Hoskins & Mirus, developed by Hesmondhalgh): when media crosses cultural boundaries, local specificity reduces its value to foreign audiences. US media minimises cultural discount by: producing for large domestic market (economies of scale), using universal genres (action, romance), English language advantage, minimal cultural specificity. This gives US content a competitive advantage globally — other nations' content faces higher discount barriers. Counter-examples: Korean Wave (Hallyu) — K-drama, K-pop have reduced their cultural discount through universal genre conventions + distinctive aesthetics. Exam: connect to global media dominance debates.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A British sitcom with jokes about specific UK institutions (NHS, the BBC, British politics) struggles to attract audiences in the United States. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"Gatekeeping","B":"Rating","C":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","D":"The cultural discount — UK-specific references reduce the sitcom's appeal abroad"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-132","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"In the context of digital media, 'user-generated content' (UGC) challenges traditional media industry models because: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"Cultivation theory — audiences are being cultivated to seek dramatic content over time through repeated, long-term exposure to sensationalised coverage across many broadcasts, which describes a slow shift in attitudes and expectations rather than a single viral clip reaching millions overnight.","B":"Stereotype controls production","C":"It blurs the distinction between producer and audience — anyone with a smartphone can create and distribute content globally, undermining the gatekeeping power of traditional media institutions and creating a competitive content environment that legacy media cannot easily replicate.","D":"Rating measures ideology"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"UGC and the 'prosumer' (Toffler): audiences as producers. Platforms enabling UGC: YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Twitch, podcasting. Challenges to legacy media: (1) Gatekeeping disrupted — publishers no longer control what reaches audiences; (2) Revenue diverted — advertising follows attention, which increasingly goes to UGC platforms; (3) Speed — UGC often breaks news faster than journalists; (4) Scale — YouTube alone uploads 500 hours of video per minute. BUT: quality control, misinformation, algorithmic curation create new problems (Livingstone & Lunt). Gauntlett's 'making is connecting' — cultural participation is democratised. Critical view: platform companies (not creators) capture most of the value; precarious 'creator economy'.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A teenager's home video of a local flood goes viral on TikTok, reaching 10 million viewers before any news broadcaster has covered the story. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"How UGC bypasses gatekeeping — the teenager is both producer and distributor","B":"Institutional ownership","C":"User-generated producer","D":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-134","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Uses and gratifications theory treats audiences as: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"Two-step flow","C":"Reception theory","D":"active users selecting media to meet needs"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"Audiences may seek information, identity, social interaction or entertainment.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Uses and gratifications theory treats audiences as:","options":{"A":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self.","B":"active users selecting media to meet needs","C":"Production budget","D":"Regulatory code"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-136","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"The hypodermic needle model assumes media messages are: (alternative wording)","options":{"A":"directly injected into passive audiences","B":"Reception theory","C":"The hypodermic model","D":"Diversion would mean the student is using the programme only to escape from everyday life; identifying with a contestant from a similar background instead illustrates the personal identity function, because the audience is actively using the text to explore and reinforce a sense of self."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-ACTIVITY","scaffold":"It is a simple effects model and is criticised for underestimating audience activity.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"The hypodermic needle model assumes media messages are:","options":{"A":"A negotiated reading — they partially accepted the message.","B":"Regulatory code","C":"directly injected into passive audiences","D":"Two-step flow"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-138","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Audience fragmentation means: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"audiences are divided across more channels and platforms","C":"Ownership structure","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Digital choice can make audiences more dispersed and specialised.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Audience fragmentation means:","options":{"A":"An example of synergy — the BAFTA award drives subscriptions.","B":"A production code","C":"Primary institution","D":"audiences are divided across more channels and platforms"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-140","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Curran and Seaton link media ownership to: (applied formulation)","options":{"A":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","B":"Genre convention","C":"concentration of power and limits on diversity","D":"Lighting codes"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-OWNERSHIP-POWER","scaffold":"Their political-economy approach examines ownership, control and plurality.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Curran and Seaton link media ownership to:","options":{"A":"concentration of power and limits on diversity","B":"Maximise audience ratings above all other considerations.","C":"Narrative order","D":"the speed of internet access"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-142","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"Van Zoonen's feminist approach examines: (comparison formulation)","options":{"A":"Convergence — different technologies delivering the same content.","B":"Genre convention","C":"Distribution channel","D":"how media construct gender and reproduce or challenge power"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-REPRESENTATION-GENDER","scaffold":"Gender representation can naturalise unequal roles and viewpoints.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"Van Zoonen's feminist approach examines:","options":{"A":"All news programmes must give exactly equal time to all political parties.","B":"how media construct gender and reproduce or challenge power","C":"Cultivation only","D":"The hypodermic model"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-144","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A cultural discount occurs when: (second formulation)","options":{"A":"a media product loses appeal across cultures because meanings are locally specific","B":"Audience isolation","C":"Cultivation — US audiences have been cultivated to prefer domestic content.","D":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-CULTURAL-INDUSTRIES","scaffold":"Language, humour and cultural references can reduce exportability.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A cultural discount occurs when:","options":{"A":"The tendency for audiences to undervalue cultural products compared to their production cost","B":"Gatekeeping","C":"a media product loses appeal across cultures because meanings are locally specific","D":"Rating"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-146","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A gatekeeper is an actor who: (exam practice variant)","options":{"A":"User-generated producer","B":"selects or filters what reaches an audience","C":"Genre removes choice","D":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"Editors, platforms, algorithms and owners can all influence visibility.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A gatekeeper is an actor who:","options":{"A":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","B":"A preferred reading","C":"Institutional ownership","D":"selects or filters what reaches an audience"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-148","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media algorithm may affect audiences by: (another formulation)","options":{"A":"Synergy — TikTok and news broadcasters benefit from sharing the same content.","B":"Rating measures ideology","C":"ranking and recommending content based on data","D":"Institutional ownership"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-DIGITAL-PARTICIPATION","scaffold":"Recommendation systems shape visibility and can reinforce existing preferences.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media algorithm may affect audiences by:","options":{"A":"ranking and recommending content based on data","B":"Genre removes choice","C":"professional institutions that create all media content for passive audiences, rather than ordinary users who create and share content themselves.","D":"A preferred reading"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"MEDIA-COV-150","spec":"MEDIA-2","stem":"A media production's target audience is: (revision variant)","options":{"A":"A niche audience is a relatively small, specialised audience group whose members share particular interests; it is not an audience that passively receives one fixed meaning from a producer.","B":"A production code","C":"A preferred reading","D":"the group the producers intend to reach"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GMEDIA-AUDIENCE-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"Targeting influences content, platform, scheduling and marketing decisions.","coverageVariant":true,"reforge":{"stem":"A media production's target audience is:","options":{"A":"Primary institution","B":"the group the producers intend to reach","C":"Self-regulation — the label polices its own content.","D":"Ownership structure"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank"}]}});
